       <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
         <span class="section-number">00</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Begin with the agent’s missing world</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">You can see the browser banner, remember yesterday’s decision, know which process owns port 8000, and recognize that parentheses are suspicious in an unquoted SQL identifier. A fresh agent can see none of that unless the system carries it into context.</p>
-        <p>This chapter is about that gap. TAC-1 moved the unit of delegation from code generation to an engineering workflow. TAC-2 asks why one workflow succeeds while another collapses into corrections. Dan’s answer is not “use a smarter model.” It is a systems answer: inspect twelve leverage points and improve the one that starved the run.</p>
-        <p>The course repository makes the argument physical. It contains a FastAPI service, a Vite and TypeScript client, a long specification, mirrored backend tests, three committed slash commands, local API documentation, and a deliberately planted filename bug. The app reports the bug in the browser but says nothing in the server terminal. A human sees the failure. The agent is blind. That difference is the laboratory.</p>
-        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
-          <p class="callout__label">The chapter’s one-sentence thesis</p>
-          <p>Agentic coding becomes reliable when the Core Four inside the agent and eight external levers flowing through it give the agent enough information, capability, structure, and feedback to complete and correct its own work.</p>
-        </aside>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Stop blaming the agent; inspect its leverage</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A browser displays a precise SQLite error. The server terminal says nothing. You know the bug exists. The agent does not. Is that an intelligence failure—or an engineering failure?</p>
+        <p>The TAC-2 repository turns that question into a controlled experiment. It is a small natural-language-to-SQL application: a Vite/TypeScript client, a FastAPI server, Pydantic models, SQLite conversion, tests, local API documentation, reusable commands, and a 749-line specification. One sample file, <code>orders_(32).csv</code>, carries a planted table-name problem. In the committed state, the browser can show the failure while the server prints no diagnostic trace. The agent begins every fresh session without the visual context you have.</p>
+        <p>Dan does not solve the bug when he first sees it. He improves the system that communicates with the agent. Then he lets the agent diagnose the same bug from its own observations. That sequence is the chapter in miniature: identify a missing leverage point, install it, close the loop, and measure whether human effort falls.</p>
+        <aside class="callout callout--tactic"><p class="callout__label">The tactic’s litmus question</p><p>Before every launch ask: <strong>With my agent’s Core Four—context, model, prompt, and tools—is it possible to complete the task I have given it?</strong></p></aside>
         <h3>What you are training</h3>
         <ul class="learning-objectives">
-          <li><strong>Retrieval</strong>Produce the twelve as four in-agent and eight through-agent points, without a mnemonic sheet.</li>
-          <li><strong>Diagnosis</strong>Name the missing lever behind silence, flailing, hidden bugs, repeated work, or expensive navigation.</li>
-          <li><strong>Operation</strong>Install stdout, run the service inside the agent, let it diagnose the real failure, and close the loop with tests.</li>
-          <li><strong>Measurement</strong>Track size, attempts, streak, and presence so “better” becomes observable rather than a feeling.</li>
+          <li><strong>Recall</strong>Name the four in-agent and eight through-agent leverage points without a cue.</li>
+          <li><strong>Diagnosis</strong>Map a failed run to missing visibility, structure, validation, planning, or reusable workflow.</li>
+          <li><strong>Operation</strong>Make the real TAC-2 server observable and let an agent fix the planted failure.</li>
+          <li><strong>Measurement</strong>Use size, attempts, streak, and presence to separate progress from a lucky run.</li>
         </ul>
-        <h3>The learning rhythm</h3>
-        <p>Every major idea follows the same route: a failure hooks the need; a diagram gives you a stable picture; a video frame preserves Dan’s move; a name compresses it; the real course files prove it; a short drill makes you act; a failure repair prevents cargo culting; a current-practice note separates durable principle from period tooling; retrieval forces recall; and a teach-back turns recognition into usable understanding.</p>
-        <div class="callout callout--warning">
-          <p class="callout__label">Accuracy boundary</p>
-          <p>The repository is the committed before-state. It contains no stdout prints in <code>server.py</code> and no sanitizer in <code>file_processor.py</code>. Those are agent-generated changes in the demonstration and lab. This chapter never presents them as shipped source.</p>
-        </div>
+        <div class="callout callout--warning"><p class="callout__label">Lab rule</p><p>Type no application code by hand and paste no error message into the agent. Your job is to create conditions in which the agent can observe, act, and validate for itself. Use a disposable standalone clone so the shipped prompts see the intended repository boundary.</p></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-sdlc">
         <span class="section-number">01</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Bet on the durable system: the phase-two SDLC</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">Models rotate, tools rename themselves, and interfaces accrete features. The work still has to be planned, coded, tested, reviewed, and documented.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook">
-          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
-          <h3>The tool-chasing curriculum</h3>
-          <p>An engineer learns every menu item in today’s agent, memorizes its exact tool roster, and tunes a workflow around one model name. Six months later the names move. Their knowledge feels obsolete because it was anchored to the surface. Dan opens this lesson by choosing a different bet: watch for the engineering structures that survive the churn.</p>
-          <p>The failure is not using current tools. It is mistaking current machinery for the underlying work. If your mental model is “press this agent button,” you have no stable place to attach plans, tests, logs, or review. If the mental model is the lifecycle, every new tool can be evaluated by which lifecycle step it strengthens.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--picture">
-          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the five-node loop</p>
-          <h3>Plan → Code → Test → Review → Document</h3>
-          <figure class="diagram">
-            <div class="diagram__canvas">
-              <svg viewBox="0 0 1040 260" role="img" aria-label="Five-step software development lifecycle flowing from plan to code to test to review to document and looping back">
-                <defs><marker id="arrow-sdlc" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="var(--accent)"/></marker></defs>
-                <g fill="var(--bg-card)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="3">
-                  <circle cx="110" cy="120" r="72"/><circle cx="315" cy="120" r="72"/><circle cx="520" cy="120" r="72"/><circle cx="725" cy="120" r="72"/><circle cx="930" cy="120" r="72"/>
-                </g>
-                <g fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4" marker-end="url(#arrow-sdlc)">
-                  <path d="M184 120 H236"/><path d="M389 120 H441"/><path d="M594 120 H646"/><path d="M799 120 H851"/>
-                  <path d="M930 194 C930 244 110 244 110 194"/>
-                </g>
-                <g fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--font-display)" font-size="var(--text-lg)" text-anchor="middle">
-                  <text x="110" y="128">Plan</text><text x="315" y="128">Code</text><text x="520" y="128">Test</text><text x="725" y="128">Review</text><text x="930" y="128">Document</text>
-                </g>
-              </svg>
-            </div>
-            <figcaption>A compressed lifecycle for phase two. Several of the twelve leverage points are lifecycle nodes, which is why the taxonomy behaves like a system instead of a shopping list.</figcaption>
-          </figure>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--story">
-          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — the course repo already loops</p>
-          <h3>Read the repository as a lifecycle</h3>
-          <p>Dan does not need a large enterprise example. The TAC-2 repository already maps cleanly. The 749-line file <code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code> is the plan. The <code>app/</code> tree is code. <code>app/server/tests/</code> supplies test. The live diagnosis and diff inspection perform review. <code>README.md</code> plus <code>ai_docs/</code> perform document.</p>
-          <p>That reading matters later. Plans, tests, documentation, and stdout are not miscellaneous productivity tricks. They make specific handoffs in the lifecycle legible to an agent. Dan’s phrase “autonomy knob to 11” is therefore not a request to skip process. It is a request to make the process executable by an agent.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--name">
-          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
-          <h3>The phase-two SDLC</h3>
-          <aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Phase-two SDLC:</strong> a compressed, focused software development lifecycle—plan, code, test, review, document—built for agents that increasingly ship on your behalf.</p></aside>
-          <p>Plain English: automation does not remove the lifecycle. It changes who carries its transitions. The engineer designs the path and evidence; the agent increasingly executes it.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--example">
-          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — classify the artifacts</p>
-          <h3>One artifact, one primary lifecycle job</h3>
-          <div class="file-map">
-            <div class="file-map__item"><code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code><p>Plan: defines scope, architecture, types, and implementation detail before generation.</p></div>
-            <div class="file-map__item"><code>app/client/</code> + <code>app/server/</code><p>Code: the two-service implementation created from the plan.</p></div>
-            <div class="file-map__item"><code>app/server/tests/</code><p>Test: concrete feedback that the agent can run and interpret.</p></div>
-            <div class="file-map__item"><code>README.md</code> + <code>ai_docs/</code><p>Document: internal operating knowledge plus local third-party references.</p></div>
-          </div>
-          <p>Review is not represented by one magic folder here. It occurs when the engineer and agent inspect diffs, compare behavior with the plan, and decide whether the evidence is sufficient. That prevents the common mistake of equating “review” with only a hosted pull-request screen.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--try">
-          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — 4 minute drill</p>
-          <h3>Map the lesson before memorizing it</h3>
-          <ol><li>Write the five lifecycle steps from memory.</li><li>Place stdout under the step it most directly strengthens.</li><li>Place plans, tests, and documentation on their matching nodes.</li><li>Explain where architecture and types help all five steps rather than only one.</li></ol>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--fail">
-          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
-          <h3>Failure: a flat list with no lifecycle</h3>
-          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you can recite “plans, tests, docs” but cannot explain why a run failed after coding or why hidden errors escape a supposedly successful implementation.</p>
-          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> ask which lifecycle transition lacked information or evidence. A plan can fail to constrain code. Code can fail to emit runtime state. Tests can fail to cover the bad filename. Review can fail to compare behavior with the plan. Documentation can fail to survive the session. The lifecycle turns vocabulary into diagnosis.</p>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--current">
-          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
-          <h3>The interfaces changed; the lifecycle did not</h3>
-          <aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agents now run background tasks, coordinate richer subagents, and expose more structured permission and review modes than the course-era interface. Those are implementation details around the same five nodes. Evaluate new features by asking which transition they make more reliable.</p></aside>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--check">
-          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Close the section before revealing</h3>
-          <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the five steps and Dan’s reason for betting on them.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Plan, code, test, review, document. They are durable engineering structures that survive model and tool churn.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Which repository artifacts make plan, code, test, and document visible?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The 749-line spec; the client/server app; server tests; and README plus ai_docs.</p></details></div></section>
-        </div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>In sixty seconds, explain why “autonomy” means making the lifecycle executable, not bypassing it. Use one TAC-2 file for four of the five nodes and describe review as an evidence decision.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Bet on the durable loop</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Models and tool surfaces change. The engineering loop survives: plan, code, test, review, document.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><h3>What remains when the favorite tool changes?</h3><p>A career strategy built around one product screen is fragile. Dan’s opening bet is on the part of software work that keeps returning through every platform shift: the software development lifecycle. The phase-two version is deliberately compressed to five verbs. They are stable enough to organize the rest of the course and concrete enough to inspect in a repository.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><h3>Five nodes, one repeatable path</h3><div class="sdlc-map" aria-label="Phase-two software development lifecycle"><div><strong>1 · Plan</strong><span>Describe the outcome and route.</span></div><div><strong>2 · Code</strong><span>Create the implementation.</span></div><div><strong>3 · Test</strong><span>Generate concrete feedback.</span></div><div><strong>4 · Review</strong><span>Inspect evidence and risk.</span></div><div><strong>5 · Document</strong><span>Preserve operating context.</span></div></div><p>The arrows matter. A plan that never reaches tests is not the system. Tests that never feed review are not a closed loop. Documentation becomes input to the next plan.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The repository already tells the whole lifecycle story</h3><p><code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code> is the plan. The <code>app/</code> tree is the code. <code>app/server/tests/</code> provides tests. The lesson’s live inspection performs review. <code>README.md</code> and <code>ai_docs/</code> preserve internal and external knowledge. The twelve points will later attach to these nodes: plans strengthen planning, stdout strengthens review, tests strengthen validation, and documentation improves the next agent boot.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><h3>The phase-two SDLC</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Phase-two SDLC:</strong> plan → code → test → review → document, progressively augmented so an agent can ship on your behalf.</p></aside><p>The goal is not to discard the lifecycle. It is to turn its nodes into programmable, observable structures and move the autonomy knob upward without throwing away engineering judgment.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><h3>Trace one change through all five nodes</h3><p>The sanitizer change begins as a diagnosis and a tiny plan. The agent edits <code>file_processor.py</code>. A focused test covers parenthesized filenames, leading digits, and invalid-only names. The full suite supplies review evidence. A reusable <code>/start</code> command documents how to run the service inside the agent next time. What looked like “fix one SQL error” becomes a complete loop.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><h3>Point to the artifact</h3><p>Without opening the chapter map, locate one TAC-2 file or directory for each lifecycle node. Then answer: which node is weakest in the committed broken state? A good answer names review/observability because the runtime failure does not reach an agent-readable stream.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><h3>A flat checklist hides the system</h3><p><strong>Failure:</strong> memorize twelve nouns and skip the lifecycle framing. <strong>Fix:</strong> attach each lever to the transition it improves. Standard out and tests create feedback. Architecture and types reduce navigation. Plans and templates improve delegation. ADWs trigger compositions. The map becomes causal instead of decorative.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><h3>The verbs transfer even when the interface does not</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Durable layer</p><p>Agent products may rename modes and tools, but repositories still need a proposed change, implementation, executable checks, reviewable evidence, and retained knowledge. Teach the lifecycle first; map product controls onto it second.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="SDLC retrieval"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the five steps and explain why Dan bets on them.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Plan, code, test, review, document. The lifecycle is a durable aspect of engineering, so it survives churn in models, tools, and interfaces.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p><strong>One minute:</strong> explain to a teammate why agentic coding augments the SDLC rather than replacing it. Use the sanitizer change as your five-node example.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-perspective">
         <span class="section-number">02</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #2: Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">The agent may be capable enough to solve the problem and still be unable to begin because the world you assume is not the world in its context window.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Brilliant, but blind</h3><p>You launch a fresh session and ask whether the server has a problem. You remember the upload, see the browser’s SQLite error, and know the terminal window where the process runs. The agent sees a prompt with none of those observations. It does not have yesterday’s session, your open tabs, or a durable memory of the repository. Capability is not visibility.</p><p>This is why anthropomorphizing continuity is expensive. Every session is a new worker arriving without the briefing. If it guesses poorly, the model may be fine; the system may simply have asked it to operate without the evidence a human would require.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the possibility test</p><h3>What reaches the blank agent?</h3><div class="core-four"><div class="core-four__cell"><b>Context</b><p>Files, history, logs, and current state it can actually see.</p></div><div class="core-four__cell"><b>Model</b><p>The reasoning engine selected for the work.</p></div><div class="core-four__cell"><b>Prompt</b><p>The outcome, constraints, and evidence requested.</p></div><div class="core-four__cell core-four__cell--tools"><b>Tools</b><p>The actions and observations it is permitted to use.</p></div><div class="core-four__plus">?</div></div><p>Put the task in the center and ask a binary question: with these four, is completion possible? Not likely, not impressive—possible.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s tactic card</p><h3>Equip the agent with what you would need</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0183-tactic-adopt-agents-perspective.jpg" alt="Tactic card reading Adopt Your Agent's Perspective with daily actions around context, logging, types, tests, and architecture" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">3:03</span>The tactic card turns perspective-taking into daily actions: provide the core four, expose logging, use consistent types and architecture, write tests, and equip the agent with what you would need.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan’s key move is to change the evaluation target. Instead of asking “Can this model solve CSV bugs?” ask “Did this agent receive the same runtime evidence, repository context, tools, and validation path I would use?” That question makes the engineer responsible for the conditions while leaving the solution to the agent.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p><h3>Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Tactic #2</p><p>Before launch, ask: <strong>With my agent’s core four—context, model, prompt, tools—is it possible to complete the task I’ve given it?</strong></p></aside><p>Plain English: stop grading the worker on information you never handed over. The new leverage question is not only what you can do; it is what you can teach the system to do repeatedly.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — prime a cold session</p><h3><code>/prime</code> is a perspective repair</h3><p>The committed <code>.claude/commands/prime.md</code> runs <code>git ls-files</code>, reads <code>README.md</code>, and asks the agent to summarize the project. It is only seven lines, but it turns a blank session into one that knows the tree, the setup, and the application’s purpose. The command does not make the agent remember. It makes context reconstruction repeatable.</p><p>The repo has no committed <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Do not imagine one. Here, the README and prime command carry the internal briefing role. That absence is pedagogically useful: it shows that “agent docs” are a function before they are a particular filename.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 5 minute drill</p><h3>Write the preflight, not the answer</h3><ol><li>Choose one real task in <code>tac/tac-2</code>.</li><li>List what you would inspect before acting.</li><li>Mark which items a fresh agent receives automatically, which <code>/prime</code> adds, and which remain missing.</li><li>State the smallest context or tool change that makes completion possible.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: blame the model for an impossible assignment</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the agent guesses about a browser-only error, searches unrelated files, or repeats questions you answered in another session.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> run the possibility test. Put the runtime output where it can be read, prime the repository state, name the target, and ensure the agent can act and verify. Only after those conditions hold does model quality become a useful diagnosis.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Longer-running agents make the tactic more important</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agents may now work farther from supervision, use background processes, and delegate to specialized workers. Distance magnifies perspective errors: a missing log or ambiguous path can waste a longer run. Preflight context and evidence before increasing autonomy.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Say the exact diagnostic</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">State Tactic #2, the three-word diagnosis that motivates it, and the litmus question.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective; “brilliant, but blind”; with my agent’s core four, is it possible to complete the task I’ve given it?</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does <code>/prime</code> do in this repo?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Runs git ls-files, reads README.md, and summarizes the project to rebuild context in a fresh session.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain “brilliant, but blind” without saying the model is forgetful. Distinguish ephemeral session state from durable repository context, then show how <code>/prime</code> bridges the gap.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Adopt your agent’s perspective</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Your agent is brilliant but blind. Anything outside its context and tools does not exist for the run.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><h3>You can see the browser; the fresh agent cannot</h3><p>A developer carries continuity almost unconsciously: yesterday’s diagnosis, an open browser tab, a terminal pane, the reason a filename looked suspicious. A new agent session carries none of it. Treating that session as if it remembers is not optimism; it is a malformed task.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0183-tactic-adopt-agents-perspective.jpg" alt="Tactic slide for Adopt Your Agent's Perspective with the Core Four litmus question and daily actions" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">3:03</span>The tactic card turns empathy into an engineering checklist: provide the Core Four, readable output, consistent types and architecture, tests, and whatever you would need to do the job.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>Prime is a deliberate antidote to ephemerality</h3><p>The shipped <code>.claude/commands/prime.md</code> runs <code>git ls-files</code>, reads <code>README.md</code>, and asks for a codebase summary. It does not make the agent omniscient. It establishes a repeatable minimum starting state. Dan repeatedly opens fresh instances during the lesson so success cannot be attributed to hidden conversational memory.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Tactic #2</p><p><strong>Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective:</strong> equip the agent with what you would need, then judge the task from what it can actually see and do.</p></aside><p>This is not anthropomorphism. It is interface design. The perspective is the set of available observations, instructions, capabilities, structures, and feedback loops.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><h3>The same prompt is possible in one state and impossible in another</h3><p>“Any issues with the server?” is useless when the server runs in an outside terminal and prints nothing. It becomes sufficient after error details reach stdout and the server runs inside the agent’s tool call. The model did not become smarter between runs. The task became possible.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Choose a task you recently gave an agent. Write four lines: context it could see, model/configuration, exact prompt, tools permitted. Then write one sentence answering the litmus question honestly. If “no,” improve the system before improving the prose.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> assume the agent remembers a previous session or can see UI-only state. <strong>Fix:</strong> create a cold-start path: prime from repository truth, run relevant processes through observable tools, and place important facts in local files or command output.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Transfer rule</p><p>Longer-running agents make this tactic more important, not less. The farther execution travels from supervision, the more explicit starting state, permission boundaries, logs, checks, and completion evidence must become.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">State Tactic #2, the motivating diagnosis, and the launch question.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective; the agent is brilliant but blind; ask whether its context, model, prompt, and tools make the assigned task possible.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain to a new teammate why a fresh agent changes the task even when the repository has not changed. Use <code>/prime</code> and the silent browser error as evidence.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
-      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-taxonomy">
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-map">
         <span class="section-number">03</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">The money map: four in, eight through</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A reliable run is a stack. Four levers travel with the agent; eight external levers feed information, structure, and feedback through its context window.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The re-prompt spiral has no vocabulary</h3><p>A run fails. You add detail to the prompt. It fails differently. You swap models, paste an error, ask for tests, and eventually get a green result. The work succeeds, but you cannot say why. Next week the same category of failure returns because the system learned nothing.</p><p>The taxonomy gives those corrections names. If the agent cannot see runtime state, that is standard out. If it cannot follow a data contract, inspect types. If it keeps rediscovering setup, inspect documentation or templates. If it declares success with hidden regressions, inspect tests. Naming turns local rescue into system improvement.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the 4 + 8 stack</p><h3>Keep the family boundary visible</h3><div class="leverage-map" aria-label="Twelve leverage points grouped into four in-agent and eight through-agent points">
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--in"><b>1 · Context</b><p>System instructions, repository state, conversation, and observations.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--in"><b>2 · Model</b><p>Language-model selection and configuration.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--in"><b>3 · Prompt</b><p>User instruction, outcome, constraints, and query.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--in"><b>4 · Tools</b><p>Available functions, actions, and capabilities.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>5 · Standard Out</b><p>Responses from commands, scripts, and application logging.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>6 · Types</b><p>Schemas, classes, interfaces, errors, and structures.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>7 · Documentation</b><p>Internal codebase knowledge and external references.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>8 · Tests</b><p>Self-validating feedback loops.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>9 · Architecture</b><p>Codebase structure and navigation boundaries.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>10 · Plans</b><p>Detailed prompts for large bodies of work.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>11 · Templates</b><p>Reusable, embeddable agentic prompts.</p></article>
-          <article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>12 · ADWs</b><p>Prompts plus code plus an autonomous trigger.</p></article>
-        </div></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — the authoritative slide</p><h3>Dan draws the boundary before the demo</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0345-twelve-leverage-points-diagram.jpg" alt="Dan's slide listing twelve leverage points in in-agent and through-agent groups" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">5:45</span>The authoritative map. The top four are always with the agent; the lower eight are external levers that eventually interact with it, usually by flowing through context.</figcaption></figure><p>The order is useful but the family distinction matters more. Context, model, prompt, and tools define the active agent. Standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, and ADWs are properties of the surrounding engineering system. They become leverage only when the agent can consume or execute them.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p><h3>In-agent and through-agent leverage</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Canonical taxonomy</p><p><strong>In-agent:</strong> context, model, prompt, tools. <strong>Through-agent:</strong> standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</p></aside><p>Plain English: the first family describes the worker you launched. The second describes the workplace you built around it.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — point to all twelve</p><h3>The TAC-2 repo is a leverage-point museum</h3><p>The core four live in the active Claude Code session and its settings. Standard out is conspicuous by its absence in committed <code>server.py</code>. Types live in Pydantic models and mirrored TypeScript interfaces. Internal docs live in the README; external docs in <code>ai_docs/</code>. Tests mirror the server core. Architecture divides client from server. The 749-line spec is a plan. Three markdown files in <code>.claude/commands/</code> are templates. Empty <code>adws/</code> and <code>agents/</code> directories foreshadow the highest abstraction without pretending it is implemented.</p><p>That last distinction matters: the repository shows the ladder’s destination, not a finished ADW. The lesson seed is <code>claude -p "/tools"</code>, a headless prompt call that can later be wrapped in code and triggered.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 6 minute drill</p><h3>Diagnose before you prescribe</h3><ol><li>For each symptom, name one primary missing lever: silent server error; generic dictionaries; repeated setup prompt; green run with a hidden bug; agent searches client code for a backend failure.</li><li>Write one intervention per symptom.</li><li>Circle any intervention that merely makes the prompt longer without changing the system.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: twelve words as trivia</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you recall the list in order but respond to every failed run with “improve the prompt.”</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> require a missing-lever sentence before the next prompt: “The run failed because ___ could not reach the agent; I will install ___ and rerun the same task.” This makes the taxonomy falsifiable. If the result does not improve, revise the diagnosis rather than praising the vocabulary.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>The taxonomy survives richer machinery</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Tool names, command packaging, and runtime control have evolved. The 4 + 8 split still works as a failure taxonomy because it describes information and feedback boundaries, not a specific product menu. New features should be placed on the map rather than appended as a thirteenth primitive.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Retrieve by family, then diagnose</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name both families and all twelve points.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>In-agent: context, model, prompt, tools. Through-agent: standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">An agent reports success but the bad CSV still fails. Which lever should you investigate first?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tests or another concrete verification loop. A claim of success without checking the planted case is missing self-validation.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Draw two boxes labeled “in” and “through.” Fill all twelve without notes. Then teach one failure path from symptom to missing lever to system change. A list without a diagnostic example does not pass.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Use the twelve as a diagnostic map</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Four leverage points live in the agent. Eight external levers gain power by flowing through its context window.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><h3>A failed one-shot is not “agent weirdness”</h3><p>When a run fails, you need a better question than “should I retry?” The twelve points create a finite search space. Was the relevant code absent from context? Was the tool unavailable? Was runtime output silent? Did generic dictionaries erase a traceable type? Was there no test, plan, reusable command, or autonomous trigger?</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0345-twelve-leverage-points-diagram.jpg" alt="Slide listing the twelve leverage points in in-agent and through-agent families" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">5:45</span>The money slide. The top band is always in-agent; the lower bands are external structures whose information and feedback pass through the agent.</figcaption></figure><div class="leverage-grid" aria-label="Twelve leverage points"><article><strong>Context</strong><span>System prompts, files, history, observations.</span></article><article><strong>Model</strong><span>LLM choice and configuration.</span></article><article><strong>Prompt</strong><span>User instructions and queries.</span></article><article><strong>Tools</strong><span>Available functions and capabilities.</span></article><article><strong>Standard out</strong><span>Command responses and logging.</span></article><article><strong>Types</strong><span>Schemas, classes, errors, structures.</span></article><article><strong>Documentation</strong><span>Internal and third-party knowledge.</span></article><article><strong>Tests</strong><span>Self-validating feedback loops.</span></article><article><strong>Architecture</strong><span>Codebase structure and consistency.</span></article><article><strong>Plans</strong><span>Detailed prompts for massive work.</span></article><article><strong>Templates</strong><span>Reusable, embeddable prompts.</span></article><article><strong>ADWs</strong><span>Prompts, code, and autonomous triggers.</span></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The demo walks the map instead of lecturing it</h3><p><code>/install</code> and <code>/prime</code> shape context and prompt. <code>/tools</code> exposes capabilities. The planted bug reveals missing stdout. <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> reveals type flow. Client/server and mirrored tests reveal architecture. Local docs reduce lookup cost. Tests close validation. A 749-line spec, reusable slash commands, and <code>claude -p</code> form the abstraction ladder.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0400-install-slash-command-run.jpg" alt="Claude install command reading the README, installing dependencies, and recovering from a failed pip attempt" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:40</span>The install command is already a composition: it executes the prime prompt, reads repository truth, installs both services, and corrects its initial package-manager assumption from the README.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Two families</p><p><strong>In-agent:</strong> context, model, prompt, tools. <strong>Through-agent:</strong> standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</p></aside><p>The first four travel with every run. The other eight are codebase and workflow structures that become useful when their information or feedback reaches the context window.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><h3>Diagnose before you reroll</h3><div class="diagnostic-grid"><article><strong>Agent cannot find the feature</strong><span>Probe context, types, docs, and architecture.</span></article><article><strong>Agent cannot see the failure</strong><span>Probe tools and standard out.</span></article><article><strong>Agent claims success with a bug</strong><span>Probe tests and review evidence.</span></article><article><strong>Workflow needs constant correction</strong><span>Probe plan, template, then ADW design.</span></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Take the sentence “the agent failed to upload the CSV.” Produce three different diagnoses using the map: one missing context, one missing observation, and one missing validation loop. Notice how each diagnosis suggests a different intervention.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> learn the list as trivia or treat all failures as prompt failures. <strong>Fix:</strong> name the observed symptom, select the smallest plausible lever, change one condition, and run again. This is an engineering ablation, not a mood.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Taxonomy over product vocabulary</p><p>Keep Dan’s twelve as the stable vocabulary. New agent products can add hooks, sandboxes, background jobs, skills, and telemetry; each still maps to context, tools, templates, observability, validation, or orchestration.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name both families and all twelve members.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>In-agent: context, model, prompt, tools. Through-agent: standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach the map without reciting it. Start from three failures—lost agent, silent runtime, false success—and show how the twelve narrow the diagnosis.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-tools">
         <span class="section-number">04</span>
         <h2 class="section-title">Tools you cannot name are leverage you cannot use</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A tool definition is an API contract. Read it with the same attention you would give a library whose behavior controls a production system.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The five-minute server trick hidden in a parameter</h3><p>Dan needs the application to run inside Claude Code so the service output enters the agent’s context. If he starts it outside, the human can read the terminal but the agent cannot. The capability appears blocked until one small tool detail changes the design: the Bash tool accepts a timeout. A 300-second foreground run is long enough to reproduce the failure while the agent watches.</p><p>An engineer who never reads the tool signature keeps babysitting the outside terminal. The agent is underused not because it lacks Bash, but because the operator knows “Bash exists” without knowing what Bash can express.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — capability has shape</p><h3>Tool name → parameters → possible workflow</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Discover</strong><code>/tools</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Read</strong><code>Bash(command, description?, timeout?)</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Compose</strong><code>start service for 300s</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Observe</strong><code>runtime stdout enters context</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Delegate</strong><code>agent diagnoses</code></div></div><p>The parameter is not trivia. It changes what evidence can cross the system boundary.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — inspect the roster</p><h3>Ask the agent to explain its own tools</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0630-tools-list-core-tools.jpg" alt="Course-era Claude Code tool list showing Task, Bash with timeout, file tools, web tools, and workflow tools" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:30</span>The course-era roster is a snapshot, not a standard. The durable move is to ask for the current signatures, read every parameter, and notice which tools delegate, modify, search, execute, or fetch.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan’s two-line <code>.claude/commands/tools.md</code> asks for core non-MCP development tools in TypeScript function syntax. Because the definitions are part of the system prompt, the agent can render its own working API. On screen, <code>Task</code> launches a subagent for keyword and file searches; Bash exposes command, optional description, and optional timeout; file tools cover searching, reading, writing, and editing.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — tool literacy</p><h3>Read capabilities, not labels</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s principle</p><p>If you do not know the tools the agent can use, you cannot maximize the agent’s capability.</p></aside><p>Plain English: “has terminal access” is too coarse. The callable surface, permission rules, execution duration, output behavior, and delegation semantics determine which workflows are actually possible.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — three tiny command files</p><h3>The starter set teaches inspection and composition</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0400-install-slash-command-run.jpg" alt="Claude Code running the install slash command while the repository command files are visible" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:40</span>The repository ships a minimal starter set of three commands: install, prime, and tools. The install prompt reads and executes prime, demonstrating prompts composed inside prompts.</figcaption></figure><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>install.md</code><p>Seven lines; reads and executes the prime prompt, then installs application dependencies.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>prime.md</code><p>Seven lines; lists tracked files, reads the README, and summarizes the project.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>tools.md</code><p>Two lines; asks for the built-in non-MCP development roster and typed parameters.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>settings.json</code><p>Defines what the agent may and may not execute; capability is always bounded by policy.</p></div></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 5 minute drill</p><h3>Read one tool like an API</h3><ol><li>Run <code>/tools</code> in the lesson repo.</li><li>Choose one execution tool and one modification tool.</li><li>Write their parameters, permission constraints, and returned evidence.</li><li>Name one workflow that becomes possible only because of a non-obvious parameter.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: memorize the screenshot</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you expect old tool names such as <code>MultiEdit</code> or treat the on-screen Task behavior as a permanent product contract.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> treat the frame as period evidence. Introspect the installed agent now. Preserve the habit—discover, read signatures, test behavior—while allowing the roster to change.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Background processes replace the foreground timeout trick</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Long-lived services can run as background tasks while the agent continues and polls output. This removes the five-minute ceiling and blocking call. The concept is identical: the agent must own or read the runtime stream it will diagnose.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Recover the enabling detail</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What did Task do on screen, which Bash parameter mattered, and where did the definitions live?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Task launched a subagent for searches; Bash’s timeout enabled a 300-second server run; the tool definitions were embedded in the system prompt and could be requested from the agent.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach why <code>timeout</code> is a leverage point in this demo without claiming the old roster is current. Your explanation must connect parameter → runtime ownership → stdout in context → diagnosis.</p></div></div>
+        <p class="section-lead">Tool definitions are an API. Read the signatures before designing the workflow.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>Dan discovers a tiny parameter in the course-era Bash tool surface: timeout. That fact later lets the agent run the application long enough for a browser action to produce server output inside the same context. A parameter that looks like reference trivia becomes the hinge of the debugging loop.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0630-tools-list-core-tools.jpg" alt="Course-era core tool list with Task, Bash, search, file, notebook, web, and workflow tools" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:30</span>The recorded tool roster is a period detail. The durable move is to inspect names, parameters, side effects, and permission boundaries before asking the agent to work.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The two-line <code>.claude/commands/tools.md</code> asks the agent to list built-in, non-MCP development tools in TypeScript-function syntax. The result exposes <code>Task(description, prompt)</code>, <code>Bash(command, description?, timeout?)</code>, search tools, file mutation tools, web tools, notebook tools, task management, and workflow control. Dan reads it as an engineer reads a library interface.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Tool-surface introspection</p><p>Ask the agent to describe the capabilities already embedded in its system prompt, then design work from the actual signatures and permissions.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p>The debugging run needs a long-lived process. Because Bash exposes a timeout, Dan can request <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh</code> with a 300-second tool timeout. The server’s stdout then enters the agent’s observation stream. The natural-language course prompt uses “300s”; do not mistake the comma in the spoken prompt for literal shell syntax.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>For each visible tool in the frame, write one allowed action and one thing it cannot prove alone. Example: Write can create a test file, but only Bash running the test can produce validation evidence. Task can delegate search, but its answer still needs verification against files.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> babysit an outside terminal because you did not know the agent could run the service, or assume a delegated Task answer is magic. <strong>Fix:</strong> read definitions, confirm current permissions, and connect each action to an observation and a success check.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Course-era → current</p><p>Modern tool surfaces may use background processes and output polling instead of one blocking timeout. Preserve the principle: make process lifetime and output retrieval explicit; do not preserve a product-specific signature as doctrine.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What did Task do, which Bash detail mattered, and where did the course-era definitions live?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Task launched a subagent for search work; Bash exposed an optional timeout; the tool definitions were embedded in the agent’s system prompt and surfaced through <code>/tools</code>.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain why reading one function signature changed the architecture of the CSV debugging loop. Include action, observation, and feedback.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-stdout">
         <span class="section-number">05</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Standard out: your agent can only see what you let it see</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A browser toast can be perfect human feedback and terrible agent feedback. Observability counts only when the worker doing the diagnosis can consume it.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The application screams; the server says nothing</h3><p>Upload <code>orders_(32).csv</code>. The browser reports an SQLite syntax error near an opening parenthesis. Now look at the server terminal: silence. Every endpoint catches exceptions and returns the error inside a response model, but committed <code>app/server/server.py</code> contains zero <code>print()</code> calls. A human at the browser knows the request failed. An agent watching the process has no event to reason about.</p><p>Dan calls this “bad agentic code.” The code does not merely contain a bug; it hides the bug from the worker expected to repair it.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — visibility is a route</p><h3>Failure must cross into context</h3><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Human-visible only</h3><p>The error stops at the UI boundary.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Server exception</strong></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Response field</strong></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Browser banner</strong></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Human reads it</strong></div></div></div></article><article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Agent-visible</h3><p>The process emits a trace the agent owns.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Server exception</strong></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>[ERROR] stdout</strong></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Agent context</strong></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Agent diagnoses</strong></div></div></div></article></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — before and after</p><h3>The planted filename exposes the visibility gap</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0715-csv-upload-error-browser.jpg" alt="Natural language SQL app showing an error uploading orders with parentheses in the filename" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:55</span>The browser can display the SQLite error, but the server terminal remains silent. The agent cannot use a message trapped in a UI it does not observe.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0835-stdout-fix-diff.jpg" alt="Agent-generated diff adding success and error print statements to FastAPI endpoints" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:55</span>The on-camera agent diff adds detailed error and success prints before returns. This is a demonstration change, not committed source.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The upload path derives a table name from the filename by replacing spaces only. The stem remains <code>orders_(32)</code>. In <code>app/server/core/file_processor.py</code>, line 26 constructs <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code>, which SQLite parses as invalid syntax. The bug is exact. The first lesson, however, is not sanitation; it is visibility.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p><h3>Standard out as the agent’s runtime sightline</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Through-agent lever</p><p><strong>Standard out:</strong> the response from commands and the text emitted by logs, scripts, and applications. Clear output can guide the agent because it flows into context.</p></aside><p>Plain English: if the agent cannot read the stream, the observation does not exist for that run. Top-level traces reduce the state space by pointing the investigation toward a failing path.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — trace the committed before-state</p><h3>The exact error path</h3><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal__bar"><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span></div><pre><code>orders_(32).csv
-  → upload endpoint derives table_name = "orders_(32)"
-  → file_processor.py:26
-  → PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))
-  → SQLite syntax error near "("
-  → response.error reaches browser
-  → no server stdout in committed before-state</code></pre></div><p>After the agent adds endpoint prints, the same failure becomes a text trace in the server process. The behavior is still broken, but the system is now diagnosable by the agent.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 7 minute drill</p><h3>Prove invisibility before adding logs</h3><ol><li>Confirm <code>server.py</code> has zero print calls.</li><li>Upload the bad CSV and record what appears in the browser versus the server terminal.</li><li>Open <code>file_processor.py</code> line 26 and predict the malformed statement.</li><li>Do not fix it. Write the stdout trace that would let an agent locate it.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: log for a dashboard the agent cannot access</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the team says the app is observable because a browser toast, hosted log service, or graphical console contains the error, but the running agent has no tool or path to read it.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> choose an agent-readable stream: direct stdout, a per-session log file, or a tool-mounted log source. For noisy systems, clean the output or filter levels, while remembering that warn/error-only views may omit successful state transitions the agent needs.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>New runtime controls; the same visibility test</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Long-running processes can stream output from background tasks rather than a blocked five-minute foreground call. The rule is unchanged: confirm the agent can retrieve the relevant event and correlate it with the task. “We have logs somewhere” is not an agentic guarantee.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Name the code and the visibility failure</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What made the server bad agentic code, and what exactly is the planted bug?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Endpoints returned errors to the UI without printing runtime traces, so the agent could not see failures. The filename becomes table name orders_(32), which breaks the unescaped PRAGMA table_info call at file_processor.py:26.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain the difference between “the application has an error message” and “the agent has an observation.” Use the exact bad filename, statement, and line. Do not jump ahead to the sanitizer.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Standard out is the agent’s eyesight</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A graceful browser error can coexist with hostile agent observability.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>Upload <code>orders_(32).csv</code>. The browser reports <code>near "(": syntax error</code>. Look at the server terminal: no traceback. The human knows something failed; an agent watching the process has no comparable clue. This is Dan’s “bad agentic code” exhibit.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0715-csv-upload-error-browser.jpg" alt="Natural Language SQL browser showing an error near an opening parenthesis while healthy product results remain visible" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:55</span>The before-state: rich human-visible feedback, silent server. The lesson asks who can observe the error, not whether an error message exists somewhere.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The committed <code>server.py</code> has zero <code>print()</code> calls. Upload, query, schema, and insights handlers catch exceptions into response error data; health reports error status without preserving the exception text; delete converts failures to HTTP errors. The routes differ, but the agentic fact is consistent: no detailed handler trace reaches stdout.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0835-stdout-fix-diff.jpg" alt="Agent-generated server diff adding detailed error tracebacks and successful response prints" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:55</span>The agent-produced after-state adds readable failure and success evidence before returns. These prints are demonstrated changes; they do not ship in the committed repository.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Standard out</p><p>The response text from commands and application logging that can flow directly into the agent’s context.</p></aside><p>Stdout is not synonymous with “print everything forever.” The course suggests session logs, cleanup, or reduced levels when volume becomes excessive. The requirement is agent-readable evidence at the right abstraction level.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p>The agent receives <code>@app/server/server.py make sure every exception prints all exception details, and make sure every successful response prints before returning</code>. After restart, the same upload produces a traceback. Success prints matter too: an agent needs to distinguish “no error appeared” from “the intended endpoint completed with this result.”</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Audit one endpoint without editing it. List: human-visible response, current stdout, ideal success evidence, ideal failure evidence, and secrets that must not be logged. If the agent can only infer success from silence, the design is incomplete.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> send logs only to a toast, dashboard, or service the agent cannot access; or paste the error into chat yourself. <strong>Fix:</strong> route concise runtime evidence to a process stream or file the agent can read, then reproduce the event while that stream is in context.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Observability, not print worship</p><p>At larger scale, structured logs, trace IDs, filtered session files, and error summaries can replace ad hoc prints. The invariant is direct machine-readable access to the evidence that narrows the state space.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Why was the server bad agentic code, and what exactly was the planted bug?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No detailed success/failure trace reached stdout. <code>server.py:60</code> turned the filename into <code>orders_(32)</code>; pandas created that quoted table, then <code>file_processor.py:26</code> executed unquoted <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code>, causing SQLite syntax error near <code>(</code>.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain why a user-friendly error response can still be agent-hostile. Use the route nuance and the difference between UI feedback and process evidence.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-system">
         <span class="section-number">06</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Fix the system before you fix the bug</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">Dan knows why the filename fails and refuses to repair it by hand. The lesson is to engineer the conditions in which the agent can discover and correct it.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>A thirty-second human fix that teaches the system nothing</h3><p>You could replace parentheses with underscores immediately. The app would work. The next silent failure would still require you to watch the browser, relay the message, locate the file, and direct the repair. You solved an instance while preserving your role as the runtime observation bus.</p><p>The more leveraged move is slower once and cheaper thereafter: expose success and failure, run the process where the agent can observe it, then ask a small diagnostic question. The agent should earn the right to solve the bug from evidence.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the closed diagnostic path</p><h3>System repair precedes application repair</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Install sight</strong><code>[ERROR] + [SUCCESS]</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Own runtime</strong><code>start.sh, 300s</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Reproduce</strong><code>orders_(32).csv</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Diagnose</strong><code>any issues?</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Repair + verify</strong><code>sanitize, rerun, query</code></div></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — two short prompts</p><h3>The agent cites the exact statement it observed</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0965-agent-diagnoses-sql-error.jpg" alt="Claude Code diagnosing the SQL error at file_processor.py line 26 after observing server stdout" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">16:05</span>After running the service inside the agent and reproducing the upload, “any issues with the server?” produces a diagnosis naming <code>file_processor.py:26</code>, the PRAGMA statement, and the unsanitized parentheses.</figcaption></figure><p>The sequence is strict. First a new agent is primed and asked to add detailed exception and success prints to <code>server.py</code>. Services restart; the upload proves the trace exists. Another fresh agent is primed and runs <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh, 300s</code>. The browser reproduces the failure while that agent owns stdout. Only then does Dan ask “any issues with the server?” and follow with “resolve: replace bad chars and escape.”</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — the inversion</p><h3>Fix the system, not the bug</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s move</p><p>Update the codebase to communicate information to the agent, then let the agent use that system to resolve the concrete issue.</p></aside><p>Plain English: invest in the repeatable sensing and validation path before spending human attention on the local repair.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — what the agent changes</p><h3>Sanitation is the outcome, not the lesson</h3><p>The agent-generated sanitizer shown in the lesson strips the extension, replaces bad characters with underscores, guards the leading character, and prevents an empty result. That logic belongs in the lab diff and its new test. It does not exist in the committed source.</p><p>Verification is behavioral: remove the stale database, rerun the service inside the agent, upload <code>orders_(32).csv</code>, and ask “latest delivery.” A working query plus visible success output closes the path. Merely seeing a plausible diff does not.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — rehearsal without execution</p><h3>Recite the order that preserves leverage</h3><ol><li>Say the stdout-install prompt.</li><li>Name the restart and bad-file reproduction.</li><li>Say the inside-agent start command and why the outside server must stop first.</li><li>Say the two short prompts.</li><li>Name the database cleanup and final query.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: repair before observation</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you prompt “fix the upload bug” while the server runs outside the agent and no stdout exists. The agent searches broadly, guesses, or happens to patch the right area without an evidence trail.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> revert to the before-state and install the missing levers. The lesson’s success criterion is not only a repaired upload. The agent must cite the PRAGMA failure from output it observed itself.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Use background task ownership today</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Start the service as a background task and poll or stream its output instead of blocking Bash for 300 seconds. Keep the discipline: reproduce while the agent owns the stream, ask for diagnosis before repair, and require behavioral verification.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Recover the prompts and the enabling conditions</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What were Dan’s two short prompts, and why were they enough?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>“Any issues with the server?” and “resolve: replace bad chars and escape.” They were enough because detailed stdout existed, the service ran inside the agent’s context, and the agent had tools to inspect and edit the codebase.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Tell the story as an inversion: Dan knows the bug, refuses the hand-fix, installs sight, hands over runtime, asks for diagnosis, then delegates repair. If your story starts with sanitation, start again.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Fix the system before fixing the bug</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The lesson’s strongest move is restraint: Dan knows the bug and refuses to repair it until the agent can see it.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>A quick manual sanitizer would make the demo app work. It would also preserve the real defect: future agents would still be blind to runtime failures. Shipping the local fix and leaving the system unchanged optimizes one incident while guaranteeing repeated human intervention.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><div class="workflow" aria-label="System-first debugging flow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>1 · Expose</strong><code>success + error → stdout</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>2 · Observe</strong><code>server runs inside agent</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>3 · Diagnose</strong><code>agent names file + line</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>4 · Repair</strong><code>agent adds sanitizer</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>5 · Validate</strong><code>upload + tests pass</code></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>After instrumentation, a fresh agent primes, runs the service with a five-minute timeout, and observes the failing upload. Dan stops the process and asks only, “any issues with the server?” The answer identifies <code>app/server/core/file_processor.py:26</code>, the <code>PRAGMA table_info</code> call, and the unescaped parentheses. The second prompt—“resolve: replace bad chars and escape”—produces the sanitizer.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0965-agent-diagnoses-sql-error.jpg" alt="Claude response diagnosing the PRAGMA syntax error in file_processor.py after observing server stdout" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">16:05</span>The payoff is causal, not magical: the agent cites the exact file and line because the system delivered runtime evidence into its context.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">System-first debugging</p><p>Improve the conditions for observation and validation, then let the agent diagnose and repair the concrete defect.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p>The demonstrated sanitizer strips an extension if present, replaces non-alphanumeric/non-underscore characters with underscores, ensures a legal leading character, and supplies a nonempty fallback. <code>orders_(32)</code> becomes <code>orders__32_</code>. The lab grades the behavioral contract, not exact implementation text.</p><div class="evidence-strip"><div><strong>Before</strong><span>Browser error; no handler traceback.</span></div><div><strong>Agent diagnosis</strong><span>PRAGMA call + special characters.</span></div><div><strong>After</strong><span>Valid table, 32 rows × 7 columns, green tests.</span></div></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Write the smallest evidence chain that would convince a reviewer the system improved: original error, agent-observed traceback, agent diagnosis, diff, focused sanitizer test, full-suite result, successful upload, and schema row/column count.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure A:</strong> fix the bug by hand. <strong>Failure B:</strong> ask “fix upload” before installing stdout. <strong>Fix:</strong> make the failure observable, run the process inside the agent, and require the agent to produce and verify its own change. If it still fails, diagnose the next missing lever.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Operational principle</p><p>Whether the runtime is local, containerized, or remote, arrange for the acting agent to retrieve the relevant output and checks without a human paraphrasing them. Human review remains; human relay is the avoidable bottleneck.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What two short prompts did Dan use, and why could they work?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>“Any issues with the server?” and “resolve: replace bad chars and escape.” They worked because detailed stdout existed and the server ran inside the agent’s tool context, with file tools available for repair.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What command/tool setup put the server inside the agent?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The command was <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh</code> with a 300-second Bash tool timeout.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Tell the debugging story in causal order without saying “the AI figured it out.” Name the three system conditions that made the short prompts sufficient.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-types">
         <span class="section-number">07</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Types are IDKs: information dense keywords</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A precise type name is a compressed map. Search it and the codebase tells a story about where information is defined, transported, transformed, and consumed.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The agent greps “data” and drowns</h3><p>Suppose a codebase passes anonymous dictionaries and names every boundary object <code>DataRequest</code>. A fresh agent asked to trace schema information must search generic words across source, tests, docs, and dependencies. The result set is large and semantically weak. Every file looks possible; the search space expands.</p><p>A distinctive name such as <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> reverses the economics. The name points at a concrete concept. Its occurrences reveal the flow without an elaborate prompt or prior session history.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — one keyword, five stations</p><h3>The type-flow spine</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Pydantic</strong><code>core/data_models.py</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>FastAPI</strong><code>server.py</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>TypeScript</strong><code>types.d.ts</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Client call</strong><code>api/client.ts</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Plan</strong><code>specs/init_….md</code></div></div><p>The language boundary does not erase the concept. The mirrored interface preserves the same information-dense name from backend contract to frontend consumer.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — a cold trace</p><h3>Zero conversation context, one precise name</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1220-type-flow-trace-output.jpg" alt="Fresh Claude Code session tracing DatabaseSchemaResponse through five repository files" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">20:20</span>A fresh instance traces <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> through five files. The on-screen Task subagent used seven tool calls, about 26.7k tokens, and 59.2 seconds—evidence that architecture and naming carry context.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan deliberately launches a clean instance and asks how the type flows through the application, requesting files, functions, and purpose. The agent follows the name from its Pydantic definition, through the FastAPI endpoint, across the TypeScript mirror, into the client API call, and back to the original specification.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p><h3>IDK: information dense keyword</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Information dense keyword:</strong> a distinctive name that points to exact codebase locations and represents the flow of a concrete piece of information.</p></aside><p>Types here include classes, exceptions, interfaces, schemas, and other structures—not only syntax labeled <code>type</code>. The test is whether the name carries enough meaning to navigate.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — verify every occurrence</p><h3>The five-file answer is auditable</h3><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/server/core/data_models.py</code><p>Defines the Pydantic response model.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/server/server.py</code><p>Uses it at the schema endpoint boundary.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/client/src/types.d.ts</code><p>Mirrors the backend contract; the file says the models must match exactly.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/client/src/api/client.ts</code><p>Consumes the response through the schema API call.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code><p>Documents the planned contract in the originating specification.</p></div></div><p>This trace is also a review technique. If a rename touches only four locations, the missing fifth is a clue. If the client mirror drifts from the Pydantic model, the shared name reveals a contract that needs validation.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 8 minute drill</p><h3>Trace before reading everything</h3><ol><li>Open a brand-new agent.</li><li>Ask how <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> flows, with files, functions, and purpose.</li><li>Verify with <code>grep -rn DatabaseSchemaResponse</code>.</li><li>Explain why <code>QueryRequest</code> is less dense and propose a more specific alternative without changing code yet.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: rename for verbosity alone</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> a long name adds words but not discrimination, or the engineer immediately performs a sweeping rename without checking the cross-language contract.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> optimize for semantic uniqueness, then use the trace to bound the change. Dan criticizes generic names such as <code>QueryRequest</code>; the point is not maximum length but maximum navigational information.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Tooling changes; precise names remain cheap indexes</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>The tool roster has evolved, but agents still search and read a codebase to trace information. A precise identifier gives that exploration a deterministic seed. Better tools compound good names; they do not make ambiguous contracts free.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Retrieve the phrase and the path</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What does IDK stand for, and through which five files does <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> flow?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Information dense keyword; data_models.py → server.py → types.d.ts → api/client.ts → specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach types as navigation infrastructure. Start with the generic-search failure, trace the five files, and end by explaining why a fresh agent can use the name even with no conversation history.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Types are information-dense keywords</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A precise type name is a search handle, a contract, and a trace through the system.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>When you inherit a feature, you often copy a class name and search the repository. The hits reveal where information is defined, transported, transformed, displayed, and specified. Dan’s reframe is that this familiar technique is a leverage point for fresh agents too.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><div class="formula"><b>DatabaseSchemaResponse</b><i>→</i><span>Python model</span><i>→</i><span>FastAPI route</span><i>→</i><span>TypeScript mirror</span><i>→</i><span>client call</span><i>→</i><span>spec</span></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>In a brand-new instance, Dan asks the agent to show how <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> flows through the app and to detail files, functions, and purpose. A Task subagent searches. The resulting answer names five files and summarizes the flow. The fresh session matters: codebase structure and naming, not conversational memory, carry the trace.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1220-type-flow-trace-output.jpg" alt="Agent output tracing DatabaseSchemaResponse through five files with a Task subagent completion" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">20:20</span>The trace took seven tool uses and about 26.7k tokens on camera. The result is useful because every location can be verified against the repository.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">IDK</p><p><strong>Information-dense keyword:</strong> a precise type, class, exception, interface, or structural name that points to concrete information flow.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><ol><li><code>app/server/core/data_models.py</code> defines the Pydantic model.</li><li><code>app/server/server.py</code> uses it in the schema endpoint.</li><li><code>app/client/src/types.d.ts</code> mirrors it across the language boundary.</li><li><code>app/client/src/api/client.ts</code> returns it from <code>getSchema()</code>.</li><li><code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code> records the intended contract.</li></ol><p>The phrase “five files” is precise; some files contain more than one textual occurrence.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Run the trace prompt in a cold session, then independently verify with repository search. For each file, add a verb: defines, serves, mirrors, consumes, specifies. If the agent’s narrative cannot be mapped to exact files, it is not yet evidence.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> generic names such as <code>DataRequest</code>, or untyped dictionaries passed everywhere. Search becomes noisy or impossible. <strong>Fix:</strong> use verbose domain names and explicit contracts. Dan calls <code>QueryRequest</code>/<code>QueryResponse</code> merely “not that good,” a prompt to improve deliberately rather than rename for style points.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Searchability is part of interface quality</p><p>Strong types still do runtime and editor work, but agentic systems add another consumer: repository search. Naming now affects token cost, navigation reliability, and the quality of automated explanations.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Expand IDK and name the five files in order.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Information-dense keyword. <code>core/data_models.py</code> → <code>server.py</code> → <code>client/src/types.d.ts</code> → <code>client/src/api/client.ts</code> → <code>specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md</code>.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Draw the five-file flow from memory and explain what information would be lost if the backend returned an anonymous dictionary.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-architecture">
         <span class="section-number">08</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Architecture is a tax on every agent boot</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A fresh agent repeatedly pays to understand entry points, boundaries, responsibilities, naming, and test placement. Consistency determines whether that cost compounds for you or against you.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The agent searches the whole world for a backend bug</h3><p>Without clear service boundaries, the upload failure could live in UI state, network code, endpoint parsing, a database helper, or a generated client. The agent opens many files just to learn where categories of work belong. Multiply that exploration by hundreds of fresh sessions and architecture becomes a token bill.</p><p>In TAC-2, opening <code>app/</code> reveals <code>client/</code> and <code>server/</code>. Before reading a file, the agent can exclude half the tree for a FastAPI SQL error. A simple boundary becomes recurring leverage.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — shrink the search tree</p><h3>One decision removes half the possibilities</h3><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Unbounded navigation</h3><p>Every concern may be anywhere.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>src/</code><p>UI, API, SQL, models, tests, and scripts intermixed.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>misc/</code><p>Unknown responsibility and inconsistent naming.</p></div></div></div></article><article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Legible boundaries</h3><p>Service, responsibility, and verification paths predict location.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/client/</code><p>Vite and TypeScript interface.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>app/server/core/</code><p>FastAPI and focused processing modules, mirrored by tests.</p></div></div></div></article></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — mirror the tests</p><h3>The tree teaches the next file</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1552-tests-mirror-server-structure.jpg" alt="VS Code explorer showing server core modules mirrored by test files in tests core" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">25:52</span><code>server/core</code> and <code>tests/core</code> mirror one another. The structure tells an agent where a new sanitizer test belongs before it searches.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan names the “agent navigation problem”: every new agent must explore the codebase again. His antidote is consistency—repeat folder patterns, names, comment structures, and responsibilities so each discovered rule predicts the next location.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p><h3>The agent navigation problem</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p><p><strong>Agent navigation problem:</strong> every fresh agent begins without a map and must spend context and tools learning how the codebase is organized.</p></aside><p>Plain English: if a repository is hard for you to traverse, assume it is hard for the agent. Consistency is a direct actor against complexity.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the nine checks</p><h3>Audit without refactoring</h3><div class="leverage-map"><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Entry points</b><p><code>server.py</code> and <code>main.ts</code> are obvious starts.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Constants</b><p>Unchanging information has predictable homes.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Types</b><p>Models and interfaces encode information flow.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>IDK names</b><p>Meaningful, verbose names improve search.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Bounded files</b><p>Files remain under roughly 1000 lines; the largest here is 415.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Agent docs</b><p>Markdown guidance is distributed near work.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Services</b><p>Client and server are separated.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Responsibility</b><p>File, SQL, LLM, and insight work have focused modules.</p></article><article class="leverage-map__item leverage-map__item--through"><b>Mirrored tests</b><p>Test paths predict source paths and vice versa.</p></article></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 10 minute drill</p><h3>Score the repository, do not remodel it</h3><ol><li>Give each of the nine checks a score from 0 to 2.</li><li>Cite one real path for every nonzero score.</li><li>Write the three highest navigation costs.</li><li>Stop. Dan explicitly says not to refactor yet; later lessons organize these assets with the agent.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: architecture sermon becomes a hand refactor</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you immediately move folders, rename types, and rewrite modules because the checklist exposed gaps.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> preserve the audit as evidence for a planned, agent-executed change later. This lesson trains perception. A rushed manual refactor violates both the course sequence and the lab’s zero-hand-code directive.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Organize agentic assets as first-class architecture</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Repositories increasingly give docs, specs, commands, skills, and workflow code explicit homes. The current-practice video below extends the same consistency principle to agentic assets.</p><p class="current-practice__source"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hGg3nWp7afg">Watch: 3 Codebase Folders</a></p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Recover the problem, boundary, and antidote</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the navigation problem, which decision halves this repo’s search space, and what is the antidote?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Every fresh agent must re-explore from zero; the client/server split halves the space; consistency in patterns, folders, names, and comments fights complexity.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name any five of the nine architecture checks.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Any five of: clear entry points, constants files, types, verbose IDK names, files under about 1000 lines, distributed agent docs, separated services, one responsibility per file, mirrored tests.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain architecture as a repeated agent-boot tax. Use the client/server split and mirrored tests as two examples, then state why the correct action today is an audit rather than an immediate refactor.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Architecture is paid on every agent boot</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A human learns a messy repository once. Fresh agents repeatedly pay its navigation cost.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>Open <code>app/</code>. The first choice is <code>client</code> or <code>server</code>. That boundary immediately cuts the plausible search space. Small structural decisions compound when agents boot hundreds or thousands of times.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Client</h3><p>UI, browser events, API client, TypeScript contracts.</p></div></article><article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Server</h3><p>Routes, models, processors, tests, runtime output.</p></div></article></div><p>A clean boundary is a precomputed answer to the first navigation question.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>Dan inventories a consistency checklist: clear entry points; constant files; explicit types and structures; meaningful IDK names; files below roughly 1,000 lines; agent-facing markdown; separated services; one responsibility per file; and tests mirroring source layout. The repo is small enough to see each decision.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Agent navigation problem</p><p>Every fresh agent must rediscover the codebase, so structure and consistency multiply for or against every run.</p></aside><p>Dan’s antidote is consistency. If a repository is hard for you to navigate, assume it is hard for an agent too.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><ul><li><code>server.py</code> and <code>main.ts</code> are clear entry points.</li><li><code>file_processor.py</code>, <code>sql_processor.py</code>, <code>llm_processor.py</code>, and <code>insights.py</code> name responsibilities.</li><li><code>tests/core/test_file_processor.py</code> mirrors <code>core/file_processor.py</code>.</li><li>The largest file, <code>main.ts</code>, remains 415 lines—below the lesson’s rough ceiling.</li></ul></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Score TAC-2 against the nine checklist items. For every “yes,” cite a file. For every “partial,” describe the navigation cost. Do not refactor; the lesson explicitly tells you to observe now and use later tactics to change with the agent.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure A:</strong> “the agent will figure it out,” which means it spends tokens rediscovering accidental structure. <strong>Failure B:</strong> start a broad manual cleanup the moment you hear the critique. <strong>Fix:</strong> audit, record gaps, and preserve the evidence for an agent-led refactor lesson.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Current-practice overlay</p><div class="video-cards video-cards--single"><a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hGg3nWp7afg"><span class="video-card__type">YouTube · optional</span><strong>3 Codebase Folders</strong><p>A later organization pattern for agent-facing assets such as docs, specs, and commands. Use it as an extension, not evidence that TAC-2 already has that final structure.</p><span class="video-card__watch">Watch on YouTube →</span></a></div></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the navigation problem, which decision halves this repo, and what is the antidote?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Fresh agents repeatedly re-explore; the <code>client</code>/<code>server</code> split halves the first search; consistency in names, folders, patterns, and responsibilities is the antidote.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">List five architecture checklist items.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Any five: clear entry points, constants, types/classes, precise IDK names, files under roughly 1,000 lines, agent-facing markdown, separated services, one responsibility per file, mirrored tests.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain how one folder boundary saves work on every agent boot. Then name one consistency benefit and one reason not to refactor yet.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-docs">
         <span class="section-number">09</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Documentation comes in two directions</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">Internal documentation teaches the agent this codebase. External documentation teaches it the services and libraries the codebase depends on. Local copies make both available without repeated discovery.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Every new plan starts with another web search</h3><p>An agent needs an API detail, searches the web, chooses a version, compresses the result, and later repeats the process in a new session. Meanwhile it also reopens scattered source files to infer how this particular project starts and which commands are safe. Knowledge exists, but no stable path carries it into work.</p><p>The repository responds with two local surfaces: a detailed README for itself and <code>ai_docs/</code> for third-party quickstarts. Local does not mean the web is forbidden. It means routine context is cheap, inspectable, and pinned.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — inward and outward knowledge</p><h3>Two doc streams meet in the plan</h3><figure class="diagram"><div class="diagram__canvas"><svg viewBox="0 0 920 330" role="img" aria-label="Internal repository documentation and external third-party documentation flowing into an agent and then into plans and code"><defs><marker id="arrow-docs" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z" fill="var(--accent)"/></marker></defs><rect x="40" y="55" width="230" height="190" rx="18" fill="var(--bg-card)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="650" y="55" width="230" height="190" rx="18" fill="var(--bg-card)" stroke="var(--ok)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="460" cy="150" r="92" fill="var(--accent-wash)" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/><g fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--font-display)" text-anchor="middle"><text x="155" y="105" font-size="var(--text-lg)">Internal</text><text x="155" y="145" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">README</text><text x="155" y="175" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">prime command</text><text x="155" y="205" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">nearby markdown</text><text x="460" y="145" font-size="var(--text-xl)">Agent</text><text x="460" y="180" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">plans + code</text><text x="765" y="105" font-size="var(--text-lg)">External</text><text x="765" y="150" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">ai_docs/OpenAI</text><text x="765" y="180" font-size="var(--text-diagram-md)">ai_docs/Anthropic</text></g><path d="M270 150 H355" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4" marker-end="url(#arrow-docs)"/><path d="M650 150 H565" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="4" marker-end="url(#arrow-docs)"/></svg></div><figcaption>Internal docs explain this repository; external docs explain dependencies. Both become through-agent leverage only when the agent can retrieve them.</figcaption></figure></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — read local first</p><h3><code>/prime</code> and <code>ai_docs/</code> cover different questions</h3><p>The 153-line README explains prerequisites, startup, structure, endpoints, security, and troubleshooting. <code>/prime</code> reads it so a cold session learns the project. The <code>ai_docs/</code> folder vendors OpenAI and Anthropic quickstarts for API-facing planning and implementation. Dan’s rationale is practical: avoid another web request every time the agent needs common knowledge.</p><p>He mentions the <code>CLAUDE.md</code> convention verbally, but this repository does not ship that file. Here the README and prime command fulfill the briefing function. Accuracy requires keeping the convention distinct from the actual tree.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — two doc types</p><h3>Internal and external documentation</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Through-agent lever</p><p><strong>Internal docs</strong> describe the codebase and its operation. <strong>External docs</strong> describe third-party APIs, libraries, and systems. <code>ai_docs/</code> is the agent-dedicated local folder for the latter.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — choose the source</p><h3>Ask the repository before asking the world</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Best first source</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How do I start TAC-2?</td><td><code>README.md</code></td><td>Project-specific command and prerequisites.</td></tr><tr><td>Which files exist now?</td><td><code>git ls-files</code></td><td>Exact tracked tree, not narrative documentation.</td></tr><tr><td>How does the Anthropic client initialize?</td><td><code>ai_docs/anthropic_quick_start.md</code></td><td>Locally vendored third-party guidance.</td></tr><tr><td>What changed in the latest external API?</td><td>Current official source</td><td>Local copies may drift; freshness is part of the question.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 6 minute drill</p><h3>Route four questions</h3><ol><li>Write two internal and two external questions about TAC-2.</li><li>Choose the first local file for each.</li><li>Mark which answer requires a freshness check.</li><li>Write three bullets you would place in a root agent guide if this repo gained one—without creating it in this drill.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: documentation means “whatever the model remembers”</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the agent repeatedly fetches setup instructions, uses a mismatched API version, or invents a nonexistent <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> name the local source, verify it exists, and separate pinned routine knowledge from freshness-sensitive facts. Documentation is leverage only when its authority and update policy are legible.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Project agent guides and Skills formalize the same instinct</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Root and nested agent guides, interoperable conventions, and packaged Skills make instruction loading more systematic. The course’s local-first habit remains: keep stable, high-use knowledge close to the code and fetch fresh facts from authoritative sources when needed.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Separate the two streams</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the two types of documentation, and what is <code>ai_docs/</code> for?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Internal codebase documentation and external third-party documentation. ai_docs stores local external references for agents so common lookups need not begin with the web.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Does TAC-2 ship a <code>CLAUDE.md</code>?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. Dan mentions the convention, but README.md plus /prime carry that role in the committed repository.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach the two doc streams using one real file for each. Then explain when local documentation is preferable and when a current official source must override it.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Documentation has two audiences of truth</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Internal docs explain this codebase. External docs bring third-party knowledge close to the agent.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>Why store API quickstarts locally when search exists? Because repeated web retrieval adds latency, uncertainty, and context drift. A local file makes a stable input available to every agent working in the repository.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><div class="comparison"><article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Internal documentation</h3><p>What this repository is, how it runs, where its parts live.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><code>README.md</code><br><code>.claude/commands/prime.md</code></div></article><article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>External documentation</h3><p>How third-party services and APIs behave.</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><code>ai_docs/openai_quick_start.md</code><br><code>ai_docs/anthropic_quick_start.md</code></div></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The 153-line README contains setup, commands, project structure, routes, security notes, and troubleshooting. <code>/prime</code> reads it at every cold start. The <code>ai_docs/</code> folder vendors OpenAI and Anthropic quickstarts so planning and implementation can use local references. The earlier install frame shows the agent correcting a failed assumption from repository documentation.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Two documentation types</p><p><strong>Internal:</strong> truths about this repository. <strong>External:</strong> third-party knowledge brought into an agent-readable local context.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p>Put “run services with <code>scripts/start.sh</code>” in internal documentation. Put an API client quickstart in <code>ai_docs/</code>. Then make <code>/prime</code> read only the internal facts needed by most runs; do not flood every session with every vendor page.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Classify four facts: route inventory, supported Python version, provider authentication syntax, and the reason tests mirror core. Decide which should be loaded on every prime and which should be pulled only for provider work.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> assume model training data is current repository knowledge, or search for a file the lesson does not ship. Dan mentions the <code>CLAUDE.md</code> convention, but TAC-2 has no <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. <strong>Fix:</strong> treat the actual README and commands as source truth; never invent a local artifact from a convention.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Progressive disclosure</p><p>Keep a concise always-read project guide, then route agents to deeper domain or vendor docs only when a task needs them. Local-first does not mean load-everything-first.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the two documentation types and what is <code>ai_docs/</code> for?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Internal repository documentation and external third-party documentation. <code>ai_docs/</code> keeps external references local and agent-readable so every lookup does not require the web.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Give one repo fact and one vendor fact. Explain where each belongs, when it should enter context, and why TAC-2’s lack of <code>CLAUDE.md</code> matters for accuracy.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-tests">
         <span class="section-number">10</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Tests give the agent self-correction machinery</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">Agents make mistakes. The leverage question is whether the system turns mistakes into concrete feedback the same agent can observe and act on.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The agent writes a plausible sanitizer and declares victory</h3><p>The function replaces parentheses but mishandles a filename beginning with a digit, returns an empty string for punctuation-only input, or breaks a previously valid case. A diff review may miss the edge. Without tests, “done” means the agent stopped editing.</p><p>A closed loop changes the stopping rule. The agent writes the change, runs the suite, reads failures, repairs, and reruns until the evidence is green. The human reviews the outcome instead of relaying every error.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — action feeds observation</p><h3>The loop closes inside the agent</h3><div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Change</strong><code>write sanitizer</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Test</strong><code>uv run pytest</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Observe</strong><code>stdout in context</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Correct</strong><code>repair failing case</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Prove</strong><code>all green + bad CSV works</code></div></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — tests inside the agent</p><h3>Do not become the error-message courier</h3><p>Dan enters shell mode from Claude Code, changes into <code>app/server</code>, and runs <code>uv run pytest</code>. The output streams directly into agent context. The tests under <code>app/server/tests/</code> mirror the core modules and total 621 lines, with fixtures for normal and invalid data.</p><p>The important detail is ownership. A human running pytest, reading the failure, and pasting it into chat is still the observation bus. A self-validating loop requires the agent to launch the check and interpret the result.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p><h3>Closed-loop and self-validating structures</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Through-agent lever</p><p><strong>Closed loop:</strong> the agent performs work, obtains concrete feedback, and uses the feedback to correct its own work. Dan’s line: if you are not writing tests, you are probably vibe coding.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — one regression case</p><h3>Test the lesson’s actual failure</h3><p>After the agent creates <code>sanitize_table_name()</code>, ask it to add a test covering a parenthesized filename such as <code>orders_(32).csv</code>. Require the agent to run the full existing suite before and after the new test. The new assertion must verify a safe identifier; the behavioral lab must still upload the file and query rows.</p><p>The unit test narrows the logic. The application check proves the end-to-end path. Neither replaces the other, and both should return evidence into the agent’s context.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 8 minute drill</p><h3>Design the loop before invoking it</h3><ol><li>Name the target function and bad input.</li><li>Predict one edge case beyond parentheses.</li><li>Tell the agent to write the test and run the full suite.</li><li>Record whether it corrected itself without you copying an error.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: tests as ceremony after implementation</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the agent writes code, the human glances at it, and tests run only at the end—or the human runs them and summarizes failures.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move test execution inside the agent’s work cycle. State the expected command and behavioral proof in the prompt. If no test covers the failure, have the agent add one before accepting the repair.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Backend tests are still the cheapest first loop</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>The course promises frontend agent testing in later lessons, but TAC-2 starts with the deterministic backend suite already in the repo. Dan’s install order holds: make runtime output visible, then give the agent tests.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Define the loop and its anti-pattern</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is a closed-loop structure, and what line does Dan use about skipping tests?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The agent validates and corrects its own work through concrete feedback such as tests and stdout. “If you’re not writing tests, you’re probably vibe coding.”</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain why running tests yourself and pasting failures is not the loop Dan wants. Then describe the exact sanitizer test and the separate end-to-end upload proof.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Tests let the agent correct itself</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Generation becomes a loop when the agent can act, run a check, observe failure, and improve.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>Your agent will make mistakes. The useful question is whether it has concrete feedback to detect them. Without tests, the end of code generation is often a confident claim. With tests in context, the claim can be challenged before handoff.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1552-tests-mirror-server-structure.jpg" alt="VS Code explorer showing server core files mirrored by tests core files" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">25:52</span>Mirroring turns navigation into a convention: the test for a processor lives where both human and agent expect it.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>Inside the agent, Dan switches to shell mode and runs <code>cd app/server && uv run pytest</code>. Test output flows into context. The shipped suite contains 621 lines across file, LLM, and SQL processor tests. In the verified baseline, 34 tests pass. That result is evidence the student can reproduce before any change.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Closed-loop structure</p><p>A structure in which the agent’s action produces a concrete observation it can use to validate and improve the next action.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p>After creating <code>sanitize_table_name</code>, the agent writes tests for parentheses, a leading digit, and a name made only of invalid characters. It runs the focused test, then the full suite. A normal single added test yields at least 35 passing tests; parametrization can yield more, so grading should require behavior and green output rather than one exact count.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Ask the agent to state the sanitizer’s behavioral contract before writing tests. Require it to identify the original bug case, two boundary cases, and the command that runs the whole suite. Review the plan; let the agent write and run the code.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> run tests yourself, read the failure, and paraphrase it back. The agent never owns the feedback loop. <strong>Fix:</strong> make test execution part of the agent’s task and require returned evidence. Dan’s sharper warning is that if you are not writing tests, you are probably vibe coding.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">First levers first</p><p>Backend tests are a high-leverage start because they are fast, textual, and easy to run in a tool context. Frontend checks can later add browser actions and visual evidence; TAC-2 deliberately leaves that for future lessons.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define a closed-loop structure and state Dan’s test warning.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The agent acts, receives concrete validation feedback, and can correct its work. “If you’re not writing tests, you’re probably vibe coding.”</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Explain why stdout and tests are the first two through-agent levers to install. Distinguish runtime visibility from expected-behavior validation.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-ladder">
         <span class="section-number">11</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Plans → Templates → ADWs: climb the abstraction ladder</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">One plan communicates a large body of work. A template makes an agentic prompt reusable. An AI Developer Workflow wraps prompts in code and gives them an autonomous trigger.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>Repeat the same good prompt three times and still own the launch</h3><p>You discover a reliable server-start sequence, type it in three sessions, and congratulate yourself on consistency. The instruction is good, but it remains trapped in your memory and manual presence. A teammate cannot invoke it by name. A script cannot trigger it. Every run pays a small reconstruction cost.</p><p>The final three leverage points are a progression in reuse. First make the work explicit enough to execute. Then package the instruction. Then make the package callable from arbitrary code and a trigger.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — abstraction adds reuse and autonomy</p><h3>The same intent moves up the stack</h3><div class="workflow abstraction-ladder"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Plan</strong><code>detailed prompt for massive work</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Template</strong><code>reusable agentic prompt in the repo</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>ADW</strong><code>prompt(s) + arbitrary code + trigger</code></div></div><p>Do not skip the first rung. An autonomous trigger wrapped around a weak prompt creates faster confusion, not leverage.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — 749 lines to one shell call</p><h3>The repository shows every rung</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1812-spec-749-line-plan.jpg" alt="The 749-line natural language SQL interface specification open in the editor" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">30:12</span>The plan is a 749-line prompt containing overview, stack, flow, architecture, types, and implementation detail. Dan emphasizes that he did not hand-type hundreds of plan lines.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1980-claude-p-programmable-mode.jpg" alt="Terminal showing claude -p slash tools returning the tool list and control to the shell" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:00</span><code>claude -p "/tools"</code> runs a reusable prompt headlessly and returns output to the shell. This is the seed of a workflow that code can invoke.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Between the frames sits the template layer. The repo ships <code>install.md</code>, <code>prime.md</code>, and <code>tools.md</code>. After starting the server repeatedly, Dan creates <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> live with one line: <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s</code>. It is created on camera; it is not committed in the source students clone.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — three exact definitions</p><h3>Plans, templates, and AI Developer Workflows</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s ladder</p><p><strong>Plan, simply:</strong> a prompt. <strong>Plan, accurately:</strong> how you communicate massive amounts of work to the agent. <strong>Template:</strong> a reusable, embeddable agentic prompt. <strong>ADW:</strong> one or more agentic prompts wrapped in arbitrary code and launched autonomously by a trigger.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — apply the three-time rule</p><h3>Promote repeated interaction into repository memory</h3><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal__bar"><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span></div><pre><code># created through the agent during the lab
-.claude/commands/start.md
-
-sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s
-
-# programmable seed from the plain shell
-claude -p "/tools"</code></pre></div><p>The three-time rule is an engineering trigger: when the same interaction succeeds three times, stop relying on memory and automate. The command makes the prompt reusable. The headless call makes it composable from code. A real ADW adds the wrapper and event that decide when it should run.</p><p>The empty <code>adws/</code> and <code>agents/</code> folders are scaffolding for later lessons. They are not evidence that this repo already implements self-running workflows.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — 8 minute drill</p><h3>Promote one repeated action by one rung</h3><ol><li>Name an instruction you have issued three times in this lab.</li><li>Have the agent create the smallest readable command file for it.</li><li>Restart the agent and invoke the command from a cold session.</li><li>Run one existing command through <code>claude -p</code> and capture the returned output.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: leap to autonomy before the primitive is reliable</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you build a scheduler around an ambiguous prompt, or collect one-off prompts in a private notes app where neither the codebase nor future agents can discover them.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> prove a single powerful prompt interactively, commit the reusable form near the project, run it headlessly, then add code and trigger. Master the primitive before composing it.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Bare commands grew into packaged Skills</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Commands can carry arguments and metadata, while Agent Skills package trigger descriptions, instructions, scripts, and resources for on-demand loading and sharing. Dan’s phrase “reusable, embeddable agentic prompts” anticipated the shape; the transparent markdown command remains the best way to see the primitive.</p><p class="current-practice__source"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU">Watch: RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL</a></p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>State the formulas precisely</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define an ADW and give both formulations of a plan.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>An ADW is agentic prompt(s) plus arbitrary code plus an autonomous trigger. A plan is simply a prompt and, accurately, how you communicate massive amounts of work to the agent.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What is the three-time rule, and what command file does Dan create?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Three repetitions signal a pattern worth automating. Dan creates start.md containing <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s</code>.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach the ladder with one sentence per rung and one repository artifact per sentence. End by explaining why <code>claude -p "/tools"</code> is only an ADW seed rather than a complete ADW.</p></div></div>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Climb from plans to templates to ADWs</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">One large prompt communicates work. A reusable prompt preserves it. Code plus a trigger runs it without you.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>Repeated small prompting feels interactive and productive, but the human remains the scheduler and state carrier. The final three leverage points are an abstraction ladder that progressively removes that repetition.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><div class="formula"><b>Plan</b><i>→ reviewed for one large job →</i><b>Template</b><i>→ reused as a command →</i><b>ADW</b><i>→ wrapped in code + fired by a trigger</i></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The 749-line specification bootstrapped the app. Three shipped commands preserve installation, priming, and tool introspection. After starting the services three times, Dan asks the agent to create <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> containing the course command text. Then he exits the interactive session and runs <code>claude -p "/tools"</code>, proving the same reusable prompt can be invoked programmatically and return control to the shell.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1812-spec-749-line-plan.jpg" alt="Top of the 749-line natural-language-to-SQL specification with overview, stack, flow, and architecture" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">30:12</span>A plan is not a tiny preface to coding. Here it is a detailed, reviewable prompt for massive work—and Dan explicitly says he did not hand-type its roughly 750 lines.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1980-claude-p-programmable-mode.jpg" alt="Terminal showing claude dash p slash tools returning a tool list and control to the shell" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:00</span>Programmable mode is the ADW seed: call a reusable prompt headlessly, receive the result, and compose it inside a larger program later.</figcaption></figure></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Three rungs</p><p><strong>Plan:</strong> a prompt for massive work. <strong>Template:</strong> a reusable, embeddable agentic prompt. <strong>ADW:</strong> one or more agentic prompts wrapped in arbitrary code and launched autonomously by a trigger.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p><code>install.md</code> is a prompt that executes <code>prime.md</code> before installing dependencies—prompts inside prompts. <code>start.md</code> is not shipped; it is created live after repetition. <code>adws/</code> and <code>agents/</code> are empty placeholders in the committed repository. They point toward the course destination but are not evidence of a finished autonomous system.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>Find one action you have performed three times in the lab. Write the reusable prompt’s starting state, action, timeout, success evidence, and stop condition. Have the agent create the command file. Then explain what additional wrapper and trigger would be needed before calling it an ADW.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> hand-type huge plans, keep one-off prompts in private notes, or jump to autonomous compositions before one strong prompt works. <strong>Fix:</strong> have the agent draft a plan for review; store repeated prompts with the repository; master the primitive before composing it.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Current-practice overlay</p><div class="video-cards video-cards--single"><a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU"><span class="video-card__type">YouTube · optional</span><strong>RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL</strong><p>A later packaging form for reusable agent knowledge and workflows. Learn TAC-2’s transparent command files first so the abstraction remains understandable.</p><span class="video-card__watch">Watch on YouTube →</span></a></div></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Give both formulations of plans and the evidence fact.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Simply, plans are prompts. Accurately, plans communicate massive amounts of work to the agent. The app’s specification is 749 lines and Dan says he did not hand-type it.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">State the three-time rule and the command it produced.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Three times makes a pattern and should trigger automation. Dan created <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> with the course’s <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s</code> prompt text, enabling <code>/start</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define an ADW and name its seed mode.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>One or more agentic prompts plus an arbitrary code wrapper plus an autonomous trigger. The seed is programmable mode, demonstrated with <code>claude -p "/tools"</code>.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Use “start the app” three times: once as a plan step, once as a reusable template, and once as a hypothetical ADW. Make the trigger difference explicit.</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-kpis">
         <span class="section-number">12</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Measure leverage with size, attempts, streak, and presence</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">A run that feels impressive may consume constant supervision and five repairs. The four KPIs turn agentic improvement from a vibe into a direction.</p>
-        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The productive-feeling babysitter</h3><p>You watch every token, interrupt twice, paste an error, approve each edit, and repair three issues after completion. The feature ships, and the session feels busy. Yet the agent handled only a small task, required many attempts, broke any one-shot streak, and consumed your full presence.</p><p>Dan’s measurement system makes that mismatch visible. The goal is not to ban exploration. It is to notice when iteration becomes the permanent operating model instead of a signal that a lever is missing.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture — four directional measures</p><h3>Push two up and two down</h3><div class="kpi-grid"><article class="kpi"><div class="kpi__direction">↑</div><h3>Size</h3><p>Increase the amount and duration of work delegated per run.</p></article><article class="kpi"><div class="kpi__direction">↓</div><h3>Attempts</h3><p>Reduce post-completion corrections toward one successful prompt.</p></article><article class="kpi"><div class="kpi__direction">↑</div><h3>Streak</h3><p>Increase consecutive one-shot outcomes.</p></article><article class="kpi"><div class="kpi__direction">↓</div><h3>Presence</h3><p>Reduce human sitting, guiding, and correcting toward zero.</p></article></div></div>
-        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — write the scorecard</p><h3>Four words replace a vague feeling</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t2072-kpis-size-attempts-streak-presence.jpg" alt="Markdown note listing the four agentic engineering KPIs: Size, Attempts, Streak, Presence" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">34:32</span>The measurement system fits on one screen. The power comes from recording each run and using a broken streak to trigger a missing-lever diagnosis.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan gives concrete ranges. Five-minute runs are a start; push size toward ten, twenty, or thirty minutes, then hours. Attempts sitting around five to ten—or even three to six—suggest missing leverage. Build streaks of three, five, and ten one-shots. Drive presence toward zero so the engineer is not the permanent babysitter.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name it — the agentic coding KPIs</p><h3>Size ↑ · Attempts ↓ · Streak ↑ · Presence ↓</h3><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Dan’s scorecard</p><p>Track how much work the agent carries, how many corrections follow, how many one-shots chain together, and how much of your attention remains in the loop.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the streak math</p><h3>Corrections multiply human cost</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Engineer</th><th>Five features</th><th>Corrections after each</th><th>Relative correction burden</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One-shot operator</td><td>5 prompts</td><td>0</td><td>Baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Iterative fixer</td><td>5 prompts</td><td>3 per prompt</td><td>Dan frames the fixer as 3× slower</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When a streak breaks, do not merely reroll. Name the missing lever. Dictionaries confusing the agent suggest types. Manual error reading suggests stdout. Short back-and-forth for massive work suggests a plan. Hidden bugs suggest tests. A repeated launch suggests a template.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try — start the KPI journal</p><h3>Measure the next three prompts</h3><ol><li>Record task size as run time and scope.</li><li>Count corrections after the agent says it is done.</li><li>Track consecutive one-shots.</li><li>Count human interventions during the run.</li><li>For every break, write the missing-lever diagnosis before re-prompting.</li></ol></div>
-        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p><h3>Failure: optimize one metric into nonsense</h3><p><strong>Symptom:</strong> a three-hour run is celebrated despite ten corrections, or presence reaches zero because nobody verifies outcomes. Another failure is treating all iteration as sin even during exploration or while deliberately increasing task size.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> read the four together. Temporary attempts may rise when size rises. The question is whether the system learns which lever converts larger work into fewer corrections and sustained streaks without abandoning evidence.</p></div>
-        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Modern telemetry can approximate the four</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Session duration approximates size; retry counts approximate attempts; consecutive green agent runs approximate streak; and human interaction events approximate presence. Dashboards help only if the original definitions remain clear.</p></aside></div>
-        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p><h3>Recover all four and the math</h3><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the KPIs with their target directions and Dan’s streak comparison.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Size up, attempts down toward one, streak up, presence down toward zero. Five one-shots outperform five prompts that each require three fixes; Dan calls the fixer 3× slower.</p></details></div></section></div>
-        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Teach the four as a balanced scorecard. Give one way a single metric can mislead, then diagnose the TAC-2 demo: stdout and tests reduce attempts and presence while allowing size and streak to rise.</p></div></div>
-        <div class="paradox">
-          <blockquote>The whole lesson used iterative phase-one prompting. That was the demonstration—and the confession. It tanks presence. The road ahead moves the same primitives into programmable mode.</blockquote>
-          <cite>Chapter close · 43:50–47:14</cite>
-        </div>
-        <p>The confession is not a rejection of the lesson. Dan needed visible, slow interaction to teach each primitive. From lesson 3 onward, the objective is to compose them so the agent works farther from supervision. What remains with the engineer is architecture, direction, planning, thinking, and the design of evidence. “English is the new programming language” works only when the surrounding system makes that language executable.</p>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Measure size, attempts, streak, and presence</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Without measurement, you cannot tell leverage from luck—or productive oversight from habitual babysitting.</p>
+        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook</p><p>A feature ships after six corrections. Another ships after one. If you record only “shipped,” both look equal. Dan’s KPIs track the shape of delegated work and the amount of scarce human attention it consumes.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t2072-kpis-size-attempts-streak-presence.jpg" alt="Markdown note listing four agentic coding KPIs: size, attempts, streak, and presence" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">34:32</span>Four words create a compact operating dashboard. The target directions matter as much as the names.</figcaption></figure><div class="kpi-grid"><article class="up"><strong>Size</strong><span>More meaningful work per handoff.</span></article><article class="down"><strong>Attempts</strong><span>Fewer post-completion corrections; aim toward one.</span></article><article class="up"><strong>Streak</strong><span>More consecutive one-shot successes.</span></article><article class="down"><strong>Presence</strong><span>Less live guiding and watching; aim toward zero where safe.</span></article></div></div>
+        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The closing confession reframes the entire lesson: Dan’s visible back-and-forth was still phase-one use of Claude Code and it tanks presence. The course now moves toward programmable mode. The uncomfortable part is releasing constant visibility while retaining architecture, direction, planning, thinking, and engineering.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--name"><p class="move-label">4 · Name</p><aside class="callout callout--term"><p class="callout__label">Agentic coding KPIs</p><p><strong>Size ↑ · attempts ↓ · streak ↑ · presence ↓.</strong> Every miss should trigger a leverage-point diagnosis.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example</p><p>Dictionaries confuse the agent: install types. The repository is hard to explore: improve architecture. You manually read errors: improve stdout. The prompt grows through repeated corrections: plan. A “successful” change hides defects: tests. You repeat the same workflow three times: template it. Each operational symptom maps back to the twelve.</p><p>Dan’s streak arithmetic is intentionally rough: five one-shot features outperform five prompts that each need three fixes; the fixer is described as roughly three times slower. The lesson is not a precise productivity study. It is a warning that correction count compounds.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--try"><p class="move-label">6 · You try</p><p>For your next three prompts, log run duration or work scope, post-completion corrections, current one-shot streak, and interventions while the run is active. When the streak breaks, write the missing-lever hypothesis before re-prompting.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--fail"><p class="move-label">7 · Fail / fix</p><p><strong>Failure:</strong> treat all iteration as bad or celebrate a long run without evidence. Exploratory work can require iteration, and attempts may rise when size rises. <strong>Fix:</strong> use the metrics as directional diagnostics. Improve the system rather than demanding perfection from the first larger handoff.</p></div>
+        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice</p><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__source">Measurable proxies</p><p>Run duration or completed scope can proxy size; retry counts proxy attempts; consecutive green runs proxy streak; human interaction events proxy presence. Keep Dan’s definitions canonical and choose instrumentation your workflow actually records.</p></aside></div>
+        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the four KPIs, target directions, and the streak comparison.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Size up, attempts down toward one, streak up, presence down toward zero. Dan contrasts five one-shots with five prompts needing three fixes each and calls the latter roughly three times slower.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What confession closes the lesson?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The interactive back-and-forth used throughout was still phase-one mode and hurt presence; the course now shifts toward programmable execution while keeping observability and engineering judgment.</p></details></div></section></div>
+        <div class="move move--teach"><p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back</p><div class="teach-back"><p>Describe a streak break, name the missing leverage point, and predict which KPI should improve after the fix. Avoid saying only “use a better prompt.”</p></div></div>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section chapter-section--wide" id="lab">
         <span class="section-number">13</span>
-        <h2 class="section-title">Graded lab · Make the agent see, repair, and verify</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">You will reproduce the lesson in <code>tac/tac-2</code>: inspect permissions and tools, prove the planted bug is invisible, install stdout, run the app inside the agent, let the agent diagnose and repair the failure, close the loop with tests, and promote repetition into a command.</p>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Graded lab · make the agent see, fix, and verify</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use only a standalone clone of the TAC-2 course codebase. Preserve the original failure, preserve your final diff, and collect evidence at each transition.</p>
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning"><p class="callout__label">Safety and scope</p><p>Do not use the lesson mirror nested inside the larger AgenticEngineer repository: it has no nested <code>.git</code>, so <code>/prime</code> can see the wrong boundary. Work in a disposable standalone clone/branch. Do not run <code>rm</code>, reset, checkout-overwrite, force push, or any destructive ablation in the graded tree. Move the stale database to a backup instead of deleting it. Never expose API keys.</p></aside>
+        <h3>Lab map · 100 points</h3>
+        <div class="workflow"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Audit · 10</strong><code>permissions + routes + baseline</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Reproduce · 15</strong><code>silent planted failure</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Observe · 20</strong><code>stdout inside agent</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Fix + test · 25</strong><code>sanitizer + green loop</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Trace · 15</strong><code>five-file IDK</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Abstract · 10</strong><code>/start + claude -p</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Teach · 5</strong><code>oral retrieval</code></div></div>
 
-        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
-          <p class="callout__label">Lab boundary</p>
-          <p>Course codebase only. Type zero lines of application code by hand. Do not paste an error message into the agent. Every source or command-file edit goes through the agent, and the agent must observe failures through runtime output it owns.</p>
-        </aside>
-
-        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup">
-          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 2.0 · Setup and permissions audit</h3>
-          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15 min</li><li>read + configure</li><li>checkpoint 0</li></ul>
-          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
-            <li>Work in the course source at <code>tac/tac-2</code>. Students using the standalone repository may clone <code>https://github.com/disler/tac-2.git</code>.</li>
-            <li>Open <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. Record the nine allowed Bash patterns plus the Write tool. Record the three denied commands: <code>git push --force</code>, <code>git push -f</code>, and <code>rm -rf</code>. Explain why policy, not trust language, protects the catastrophic boundary.</li>
-            <li>Note that this lesson’s allowlist does not add the git commit or checkout permissions used in TAC-1. Infer the focus: runtime visibility and leverage, not a branch-and-commit workflow.</li>
-            <li>In <code>app/server</code>, copy <code>.env.sample</code> to <code>.env</code> and provide an OpenAI and/or Anthropic API key. Never include secrets in screenshots or agent prompts.</li>
-            <li>Launch Claude Code at the repo root and run <code>/install</code>. Watch <code>install.md</code> read and execute <code>prime.md</code>, then install frontend and backend dependencies.</li>
-            <li>In a separate terminal run <code>./scripts/start.sh</code>. Verify the frontend at <code>localhost:5173</code>, backend at <code>localhost:8000</code>, and API docs at <code>/docs</code>. Upload <code>products.csv</code> and ask “list top 5 products by price.”</li>
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 0 · Standalone setup and audit · 10 points</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15–25 min</li><li>Expected baseline: 34 tests pass</li><li>No live API key needed for core upload/test path</li></ul>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Clone the course repository into a disposable standalone directory and enter it. The course URL is <code>https://github.com/disler/tac-2.git</code>.</li>
+            <li>Audit <code>.claude/settings.json</code>. Record nine allowed Bash patterns—mkdir, uv, find, chmod, mv, grep, npm, ls, cp—plus Write. Record the three denies: force-push long form, force-push short form, and recursive force removal.</li>
+            <li>From <code>app/server</code>, run <code>uv sync --all-extras</code> and <code>uv run pytest</code>. Capture the baseline: <strong>34 passed</strong> in the verified course state. From <code>app/client</code>, run <code>npm install</code>.</li>
+            <li>Copy <code>app/server/.env.sample</code> to <code>.env</code>. The core upload, schema, health, and test work is deterministic without a live provider key. Add at least one real key only if you choose to run the optional natural-language query; never include it in evidence.</li>
+            <li>Run <code>./scripts/start.sh</code> at repository root. Verify frontend on port 5173, backend on 8000, and API docs at 8000/docs. Record all six routes: upload, query, schema, insights, health, and delete table.</li>
           </ol>
-          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 0.</strong> Without looking, name the three shipped commands, what <code>/prime</code> does, the denied commands, and the application routes: upload, query, schema, insights, health, plus delete-table.</div>
-          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>The healthy app starts and returns product results.</li><li>Your permissions explanation distinguishes allow, deny, and absent permissions.</li><li>You can explain install → prime as prompts composed inside prompts.</li></ul></div>
+          <aside class="checkpoint"><p class="callout__label">Checkpoint A</p><p>Submit the permission audit, six-route list, and baseline test output. Explain why the explicit deny list is a more useful safety artifact than “be careful.”</p></aside>
         </section>
 
-        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-see">
-          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 2.1 · Prove the planted bug is bad agentic code</h3>
-          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15 min</li><li>read + reproduce</li><li>checkpoint 1</li></ul>
-          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
-            <li>Run <code>/tools</code>. Record what Task does in this course-era output, Bash’s three parameters, and the tools capable of changing files.</li>
-            <li>Upload <code>app/client/public/sample-data/orders_(32).csv</code>. Capture the contrast: the browser reports <code>Error converting CSV to SQLite: near "(": syntax error</code>; the server prints nothing.</li>
-            <li>Inspect <code>app/server/server.py</code> and confirm the committed before-state has zero print calls. Endpoints place exception strings in response models.</li>
-            <li>Before reading the processor, predict why the filename is dangerous. Then inspect <code>app/server/core/file_processor.py:26</code> and find <code>PRAGMA table_info({table_name})</code>.</li>
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-reproduce"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 1 · Reproduce and explain the silent failure · 15 points</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>10–15 min</li><li>Uses <code>orders_(32).csv</code></li><li>No code changes yet</li></ul>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Upload <code>app/client/public/sample-data/orders_(32).csv</code>. Capture the browser error: <code>Error converting CSV to SQLite: near "(": syntax error</code>.</li>
+            <li>Capture the corresponding server terminal. Confirm there is no detailed handler traceback or success trace.</li>
+            <li>Explain the actual chain: <code>server.py:60</code> derives <code>orders_(32)</code>; pandas creates/replaces the oddly named table at <code>file_processor.py:22</code>; unquoted <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code> fails at line 26.</li>
+            <li>Define “bad agentic code” in your own words without claiming every route handles errors identically. The shared problem is missing detailed stdout evidence.</li>
           </ol>
-          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 1.</strong> Define bad agentic code in your own words and state the exact failure: <code>orders_(32)</code> reaches the unescaped PRAGMA call at line 26.</div>
-          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>You have not fixed anything.</li><li>You can distinguish browser-visible feedback from agent-readable stdout.</li><li>Your diagnosis cites the real path, line, statement, and filename.</li></ul></div>
+          <aside class="checkpoint"><p class="callout__label">Checkpoint B</p><p>Browser capture + silent terminal capture + three-step line-level explanation. No error text may be pasted to the agent later.</p></aside>
         </section>
 
-        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-system">
-          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 2.2 · Install sight, then let the agent fix the bug</h3>
-          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>25 min</li><li>agent edits + runtime</li><li>checkpoint 2</li></ul>
-          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
-            <li>Start a fresh agent, run <code>/prime</code>, and prompt: <code>@app/server/server.py make sure every exception prints all exception details, and make sure every successful response prints before returning</code>. Enable auto-accept edits as Dan does, but review the resulting diff.</li>
-            <li>Restart the outside services and re-upload the bad file. Confirm an <code>[ERROR]</code> trace now reaches server stdout. Also inspect visible <code>[SUCCESS]</code> events so the agent can reason about state transitions, not failures only.</li>
-            <li>Stop the outside services so ports 5173 and 8000 are free. Start another fresh agent, run <code>/prime</code>, then prompt exactly: <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh, 300s</code>.</li>
-            <li>While that process runs inside the agent, re-upload <code>orders_(32).csv</code> in the browser. Stop the run after the failure is captured.</li>
-            <li>Ask <code>any issues with the server?</code>. Require the answer to cite <code>file_processor.py:26</code>, the PRAGMA statement, and unsanitized parentheses from stdout the agent observed.</li>
-            <li>Ask <code>resolve: replace bad chars and escape</code>. Review the agent-created sanitizer for extension removal, bad-character replacement, a legal leading character, and a nonempty fallback.</li>
-            <li>Delete the stale <code>app/server/db/database.db</code>, relaunch inside the agent, upload the bad CSV, and ask “latest delivery.” Confirm sensible rows and success output.</li>
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-observe"><h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 2 · Install the stdout lever · 20 points</h3><ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>20–30 min</li><li>All edits through the agent</li><li>Agent must observe its own runtime</li></ul>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Start a fresh agent at repository root and run <code>/prime</code>. Ask it to instrument every endpoint’s success and failure path with useful stdout, including exception details/traceback. Grade readable evidence and secret hygiene, not exact bracket formatting.</li>
+            <li>Review the diff. Restart, reproduce the upload, and prove the error now appears in server output.</li>
+            <li>Stop the outside server so ports 5173 and 8000 are free. In a fresh primed agent, ask it to run <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh</code> with a 300-second Bash tool timeout. The comma in Dan’s spoken prompt is not shell syntax; the command and timeout are separate tool-call properties.</li>
+            <li>Reproduce the upload while the process is inside the agent. Stop the run and ask <code>any issues with the server?</code>. The answer must independently cite the PRAGMA path and special characters. You may not paste or paraphrase the error.</li>
           </ol>
-          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 2.</strong> Name the conditions that made the two short prompts sufficient: detailed stdout existed; the service ran inside agent context; the agent had tools and permissions to inspect and edit; and the failing case was reproducible.</div>
-          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>The bad file fails before and succeeds after.</li><li>The agent—not you—reads the runtime error and names the exact line.</li><li>No application code or sanitizer is hand-typed.</li><li>The final proof includes upload, query, and stdout evidence.</li></ul></div>
+          <aside class="checkpoint"><p class="callout__label">Checkpoint C</p><p>Submit the instrumentation diff, agent-visible traceback, and independent diagnosis. If the agent cannot diagnose it, check port ownership, output, and timeout before changing models.</p></aside>
         </section>
 
-        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-close">
-          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 2.3 · Trace a type, close the loop, mint a template</h3>
-          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>20 min</li><li>trace + test + package</li><li>checkpoint 3</li></ul>
-          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
