+        <p class="section-lead">Close the chapter and answer aloud before opening any disclosure. Recognition is not mastery.</p>
+        <h3>Round 1 · Framework</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Reconstruct the SDLC and map one leverage point to each node.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Plan, code, test, review, document. Example mapping: plans → plan; tools → code/action; tests → test; stdout → review evidence; docs → document/next context.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Reconstruct the 4+8 map without looking.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>In-agent: context, model, prompt, tools. Through-agent: stdout, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is the model not the first diagnosis in the planted bug?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The same model succeeds after runtime visibility reaches it. Standard out and tool context, not reasoning capacity, were the demonstrated bottleneck.</p></details></div></section>
+        <h3>Round 2 · Causal demo</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Explain the exact filename-to-PRAGMA failure chain.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The upload route derives <code>orders_(32)</code>; pandas creates the table; unquoted <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code> treats parentheses as syntax and fails.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Why are success logs part of the fix?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>They distinguish verified completion from mere absence of visible failure and give the agent top-level evidence about the path that ran.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. What evidence proves the fix rather than merely describes it?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Agent-observed traceback and diagnosis, reviewed diff, focused tests, full green suite, successful upload, and schema showing a valid table with 32 rows and 7 columns.</p></details></div></section>
+        <h3>Round 3 · Navigation and loops</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. How do types, architecture, docs, and tests work together on a cold boot?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Docs establish orientation; architecture narrows search; IDK types trace the feature; mirrored tests supply expected behavior and executable feedback.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. What is the difference between an agent-readable log and a closed loop?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A log is an observation. A closed loop connects observation to a check and a next corrective action performed by the agent.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. Why is “do not refactor yet” part of architecture mastery?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The lesson trains diagnosis and awareness. Premature manual refactoring violates the course’s move toward agent-led systematic change and expands scope before the current levers are learned.</p></details></div></section>
+        <h3>Round 4 · Abstraction and measurement</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. Distinguish a plan, template, and ADW in one sentence each.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A plan communicates one large body of work. A template makes an agentic prompt reusable. An ADW composes prompt(s) in code and launches them through an autonomous trigger.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. Attempts rose when size rose. Is the system necessarily worse?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. Larger delegation can temporarily increase attempts. Diagnose the failures, install missing levers, and watch whether attempts fall at the new size.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. What does presence measure that run duration does not?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>How much live human guiding, watching, and correction the run requires, not merely how long the compute runs.</p></details></div></section>
+        <aside class="callout callout--why"><p class="callout__label">Capstone teach-back</p><p>In three minutes, teach a junior engineer the entire lesson through the CSV bug. You must use the phrases <em>brilliant but blind</em>, <em>system-first</em>, <em>information-dense keyword</em>, <em>closed loop</em>, and all four KPI directions. A listener should understand why the agent succeeded without attributing the result to magic.</p></aside>
       </section>
 
       <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
         <span class="section-number">15</span>
         <h2 class="section-title">TAC-2 glossary</h2>
-        <p class="section-lead">Use these terms as operational language. Each should point to a concrete artifact, failure, or measurement in the lesson.</p>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use these terms as diagnostic instruments, not slogans.</p>
         <dl class="glossary">
-          <div><dt>Phase-two SDLC</dt><dd>Plan, code, test, review, document—the durable lifecycle several leverage points strengthen or directly represent.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective</dt><dd>Tactic #2: evaluate whether the agent’s context, model, prompt, and tools make the assigned work possible.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Brilliant, but blind</dt><dd>The agent is capable but ephemeral and limited to the context and observations the system supplies.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>In-agent leverage</dt><dd>The Core Four always present in the active agent: context, model, prompt, tools.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Through-agent leverage</dt><dd>External structures that interact with the agent, usually through context: stdout, types, docs, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Standard out</dt><dd>Text emitted by commands, scripts, and applications that can carry runtime state into agent context.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Bad agentic code</dt><dd>Code whose important failures are invisible to the agent expected to diagnose or repair them.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>IDK</dt><dd>Information dense keyword: a distinctive identifier that points to exact locations and a concrete information flow.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Agent navigation problem</dt><dd>The repeated cost each fresh agent pays to rediscover entry points, boundaries, naming, responsibilities, and verification paths.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Internal documentation</dt><dd>Knowledge about this repository: setup, architecture, commands, conventions, and nearby operating guidance.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>External documentation</dt><dd>Knowledge about third-party APIs, libraries, and systems; <code>ai_docs/</code> stores local references for agents.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Closed loop</dt><dd>A structure where the agent performs work, runs a check, reads concrete feedback, and corrects itself.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Plan</dt><dd>Simply a prompt; accurately, the mechanism for communicating massive amounts of work to an agent.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Template</dt><dd>A reusable, embeddable agentic prompt. The lesson uses repository command files as the transparent form.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Three-time rule</dt><dd>Three successful repetitions make a pattern; the engineering response is to automate or package it.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>ADW</dt><dd>AI Developer Workflow: one or more agentic prompts, arbitrary wrapper code, and an autonomous trigger.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Programmable mode</dt><dd><code>claude -p</code>: execute a prompt or command headlessly and return the result to the caller.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>YOLO mode</dt><dd>Dan’s name for auto-accepting edits so the agent can proceed without constant approval; it does not erase permission policy.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Size</dt><dd>How much work or run duration is delegated; target upward.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Attempts</dt><dd>Corrections after an agent claims completion; target downward toward one successful prompt.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Streak</dt><dd>Consecutive one-shot outcomes; target upward, diagnosing the missing lever when the streak breaks.</dd></div>
-          <div><dt>Presence</dt><dd>Human sitting, guiding, and correcting during agent work; target downward toward zero without sacrificing evidence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Phase-two SDLC</dt><dd>Plan, code, test, review, document—the durable five-node framework TAC progressively augments and automates.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Adopt Your Agent’s Perspective</dt><dd>Tactic #2: judge the task from the observations, instructions, tools, structures, and feedback available to the agent.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>In-agent leverage</dt><dd>Context, model, prompt, and tools—the Core Four that are always part of an agent run.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Through-agent leverage</dt><dd>External codebase/workflow structures whose information or feedback flows through the agent: stdout, types, docs, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Standard out</dt><dd>Command and application output available to the agent as direct runtime evidence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Bad agentic code</dt><dd>Code whose operation may be understandable to a human UI user but does not expose enough usable evidence for an agent to diagnose and verify.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>System-first debugging</dt><dd>Improve observation and validation conditions before asking an agent to repair the immediate defect.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>IDK</dt><dd>Information-dense keyword: a precise structural name that lets humans and agents trace information through a repository.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agent navigation problem</dt><dd>The repeated cost every fresh agent pays to rediscover the repository’s structure and conventions.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Internal documentation</dt><dd>Repository-specific truth: setup, structure, commands, decisions, and operating instructions.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>External documentation</dt><dd>Third-party knowledge, often vendored locally so agents can retrieve it without repeated web requests.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Closed-loop structure</dt><dd>A sequence in which agent action produces feedback the agent can evaluate and use for correction.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Plan</dt><dd>Simply a prompt; accurately, the way massive work is communicated to an agent for review and execution.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Template</dt><dd>A reusable, embeddable agentic prompt such as a repository command.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Three-time rule</dt><dd>When an action repeats three times, treat the pattern as a candidate for automation.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>ADW</dt><dd>AI Developer Workflow: one or more agentic prompts wrapped in arbitrary code and launched autonomously by a trigger.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Size</dt><dd>The amount of meaningful work handed off per prompt or run; target direction is up.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Attempts</dt><dd>Post-completion corrections needed to reach success; target direction is down toward one.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Streak</dt><dd>Consecutive one-shot successful runs; target direction is up.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Presence</dt><dd>Live human attention spent guiding, watching, and correcting a run; target direction is down toward zero where appropriate.</dd></div>
         </dl>
       </section>
 
