106:          <p>The repository is the committed before-state. It contains no stdout prints in <code>server.py</code> and no sanitizer in <code>file_processor.py</code>. Those are agent-generated changes in the demonstration and lab. This chapter never presents them as shipped source.</p>
179:          <aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agents now run background tasks, coordinate richer subagents, and expose more structured permission and review modes than the course-era interface. Those are implementation details around the same five nodes. Evaluate new features by asking which transition they make more reliable.</p></aside>
199:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Longer-running agents make the tactic more important</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agents may now work farther from supervision, use background processes, and delegate to specialized workers. Distance magnifies perspective errors: a missing log or ambiguous path can waste a longer run. Preflight context and evidence before increasing autonomy.</p></aside></div>
228:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>The taxonomy survives richer machinery</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Tool names, command packaging, and runtime control have evolved. The 4 + 8 split still works as a failure taxonomy because it describes information and feedback boundaries, not a specific product menu. New features should be placed on the map rather than appended as a thirteenth primitive.</p></aside></div>
239:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — inspect the roster</p><h3>Ask the agent to explain its own tools</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0630-tools-list-core-tools.jpg" alt="Course-era Claude Code tool list showing Task, Bash with timeout, file tools, web tools, and workflow tools" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:30</span>The course-era roster is a snapshot, not a standard. The durable move is to ask for the current signatures, read every parameter, and notice which tools delegate, modify, search, execute, or fetch.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan’s two-line <code>.claude/commands/tools.md</code> asks for core non-MCP development tools in TypeScript function syntax. Because the definitions are part of the system prompt, the agent can render its own working API. On screen, <code>Task</code> launches a subagent for keyword and file searches; Bash exposes command, optional description, and optional timeout; file tools cover searching, reading, writing, and editing.</p></div>
244:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Background processes replace the foreground timeout trick</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Long-lived services can run as background tasks while the agent continues and polls output. This removes the five-minute ceiling and blocking call. The concept is identical: the agent must own or read the runtime stream it will diagnose.</p></aside></div>
253:        <div class="move move--hook"><p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p><h3>The application screams; the server says nothing</h3><p>Upload <code>orders_(32).csv</code>. The browser reports an SQLite syntax error near an opening parenthesis. Now look at the server terminal: silence. Every endpoint catches exceptions and returns the error inside a response model, but committed <code>app/server/server.py</code> contains zero <code>print()</code> calls. A human at the browser knows the request failed. An agent watching the process has no event to reason about.</p><p>Dan calls this “bad agentic code.” The code does not merely contain a bug; it hides the bug from the worker expected to repair it.</p></div>
255:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — before and after</p><h3>The planted filename exposes the visibility gap</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0715-csv-upload-error-browser.jpg" alt="Natural language SQL app showing an error uploading orders with parentheses in the filename" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:55</span>The browser can display the SQLite error, but the server terminal remains silent. The agent cannot use a message trapped in a UI it does not observe.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0835-stdout-fix-diff.jpg" alt="Agent-generated diff adding success and error print statements to FastAPI endpoints" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:55</span>The on-camera agent diff adds detailed error and success prints before returns. This is a demonstration change, not committed source.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The upload path derives a table name from the filename by replacing spaces only. The stem remains <code>orders_(32)</code>. In <code>app/server/core/file_processor.py</code>, line 26 constructs <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code>, which SQLite parses as invalid syntax. The bug is exact. The first lesson, however, is not sanitation; it is visibility.</p></div>
257:        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — trace the committed before-state</p><h3>The exact error path</h3><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal__bar"><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span><span class="terminal__dot"></span></div><pre><code>orders_(32).csv
263:  → no server stdout in committed before-state</code></pre></div><p>After the agent adds endpoint prints, the same failure becomes a text trace in the server process. The behavior is still broken, but the system is now diagnosable by the agent.</p></div>
266:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Structured telemetry is richer; accessibility still decides leverage</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Logs may be structured, streamed from background tasks, or queried through observability tools. The rule is unchanged: confirm the agent can retrieve the relevant event and correlate it with the task. “We have logs somewhere” is not an agentic guarantee.</p></aside></div>
282:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Use background task ownership today</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Start the service as a background task and poll or stream its output instead of blocking Bash for 300 seconds. Keep the discipline: reproduce while the agent owns the stream, ask for diagnosis before repair, and require behavioral verification.</p></aside></div>
298:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Semantic search helps; names remain cheap indexes</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agents can combine grep, language-server references, code graphs, and semantic retrieval. A precise identifier still gives the cheapest deterministic seed. Rich tooling compounds good names; it does not make ambiguous contracts free.</p></aside></div>
314:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Organize agentic assets as first-class architecture</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Repositories increasingly give docs, specs, commands, skills, and workflow code explicit homes. The current-practice video below extends the same consistency principle to agentic assets.</p><p class="current-practice__source"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hGg3nWp7afg">Watch: 3 Codebase Folders</a></p></aside></div>
325:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — read local first</p><h3><code>/prime</code> and <code>ai_docs/</code> cover different questions</h3><p>The 153-line README explains prerequisites, startup, structure, endpoints, security, and troubleshooting. <code>/prime</code> reads it so a cold session learns the project. The <code>ai_docs/</code> folder vendors OpenAI and Anthropic quickstarts for API-facing planning and implementation. Dan’s rationale is practical: avoid another web request every time the agent needs common knowledge.</p><p>He mentions the <code>CLAUDE.md</code> convention verbally, but this repository does not ship that file. Here the README and prime command fulfill the briefing function. Accuracy requires keeping the convention distinct from the actual tree.</p></div>
330:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Project agent guides and Skills formalize the same instinct</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Root and nested agent guides, interoperable conventions, and packaged Skills make instruction loading more systematic. The course’s local-first habit remains: keep stable, high-use knowledge close to the code and fetch fresh facts from authoritative sources when needed.</p></aside></div>
346:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Backend tests are still the cheapest first loop</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Agents can now combine unit tests, type checks, linters, browser automation, and production-like traces. Start where feedback is deterministic and cheap. Dan’s install order holds: make runtime output visible, then give the agent tests.</p></aside></div>
357:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — 749 lines to one shell call</p><h3>The repository shows every rung</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1812-spec-749-line-plan.jpg" alt="The 749-line natural language SQL interface specification open in the editor" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">30:12</span>The plan is a 749-line prompt containing overview, stack, flow, architecture, types, and implementation detail. Dan emphasizes that he did not hand-type hundreds of plan lines.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1980-claude-p-programmable-mode.jpg" alt="Terminal showing claude -p slash tools returning the tool list and control to the shell" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:00</span><code>claude -p "/tools"</code> runs a reusable prompt headlessly and returns output to the shell. This is the seed of a workflow that code can invoke.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Between the frames sits the template layer. The repo ships <code>install.md</code>, <code>prime.md</code>, and <code>tools.md</code>. After starting the server repeatedly, Dan creates <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> live with one line: <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s</code>. It is created on camera; it is not committed in the source students clone.</p></div>
368:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Bare commands grew into packaged Skills</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Commands can carry arguments and metadata, while Agent Skills package trigger descriptions, instructions, scripts, and resources for on-demand loading and sharing. Dan’s phrase “reusable, embeddable agentic prompts” anticipated the shape; the transparent markdown command remains the best way to see the primitive.</p><p class="current-practice__source"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU">Watch: RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL</a></p></aside></div>
384:        <div class="move move--current"><p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p><h3>Modern telemetry can approximate the four</h3><aside class="current-practice"><p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p><p>Session duration approximates size; retry counts approximate attempts; consecutive green agent runs approximate streak; and human interaction events approximate presence. Dashboards help only if the original definitions remain clear.</p></aside></div>
423:            <li>Run <code>/tools</code>. Record what Task does in this course-era output, Bash’s three parameters, and the tools capable of changing files.</li>
425:            <li>Inspect <code>app/server/server.py</code> and confirm the committed before-state has zero print calls. Endpoints place exception strings in response models.</li>
481:            <li>If the service dies after five minutes, the course-era timeout behaved as configured. Rerun or use the minted command.</li>
503:          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Which command inspects the tool roster, and which Bash parameter enables the course-era inside-agent server run?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>/tools</code>; the timeout parameter, used for a 300-second run.</p></details></div>

