/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n '<img ' IndyDevDan/book/vol-1-tac/02-the-12-leverage-points.html" in /Users/kvnkishore/WebstormProjects/AgenticEngineer
 succeeded in 0ms:
64:          <img src="../media/generated/plate-12-leverage-points.png" alt="Editorial plate representing twelve connected leverage points around an agentic engineering system" width="1024" height="1536" fetchpriority="high">
194:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — Dan’s tactic card</p><h3>Equip the agent with what you would need</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0183-tactic-adopt-agents-perspective.jpg" alt="Tactic card reading Adopt Your Agent's Perspective with daily actions around context, logging, types, tests, and architecture" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">3:03</span>The tactic card turns perspective-taking into daily actions: provide the core four, expose logging, use consistent types and architecture, write tests, and equip the agent with what you would need.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan’s key move is to change the evaluation target. Instead of asking “Can this model solve CSV bugs?” ask “Did this agent receive the same runtime evidence, repository context, tools, and validation path I would use?” That question makes the engineer responsible for the conditions while leaving the solution to the agent.</p></div>
223:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — the authoritative slide</p><h3>Dan draws the boundary before the demo</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0345-twelve-leverage-points-diagram.jpg" alt="Dan's slide listing twelve leverage points in in-agent and through-agent groups" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">5:45</span>The authoritative map. The top four are always with the agent; the lower eight are external levers that eventually interact with it, usually by flowing through context.</figcaption></figure><p>The order is useful but the family distinction matters more. Context, model, prompt, and tools define the active agent. Standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, and ADWs are properties of the surrounding engineering system. They become leverage only when the agent can consume or execute them.</p></div>
239:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — inspect the roster</p><h3>Ask the agent to explain its own tools</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0630-tools-list-core-tools.jpg" alt="Course-era Claude Code tool list showing Task, Bash with timeout, file tools, web tools, and workflow tools" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:30</span>The course-era roster is a snapshot, not a standard. The durable move is to ask for the current signatures, read every parameter, and notice which tools delegate, modify, search, execute, or fetch.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan’s two-line <code>.claude/commands/tools.md</code> asks for core non-MCP development tools in TypeScript function syntax. Because the definitions are part of the system prompt, the agent can render its own working API. On screen, <code>Task</code> launches a subagent for keyword and file searches; Bash exposes command, optional description, and optional timeout; file tools cover searching, reading, writing, and editing.</p></div>
241:        <div class="move move--example"><p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — three tiny command files</p><h3>The starter set teaches inspection and composition</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0400-install-slash-command-run.jpg" alt="Claude Code running the install slash command while the repository command files are visible" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:40</span>The repository ships a minimal starter set of three commands: install, prime, and tools. The install prompt reads and executes prime, demonstrating prompts composed inside prompts.</figcaption></figure><div class="file-map"><div class="file-map__item"><code>install.md</code><p>Seven lines; reads and executes the prime prompt, then installs application dependencies.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>prime.md</code><p>Seven lines; lists tracked files, reads the README, and summarizes the project.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>tools.md</code><p>Two lines; asks for the built-in non-MCP development roster and typed parameters.</p></div><div class="file-map__item"><code>settings.json</code><p>Defines what the agent may and may not execute; capability is always bounded by policy.</p></div></div></div>
255:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — before and after</p><h3>The planted filename exposes the visibility gap</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0715-csv-upload-error-browser.jpg" alt="Natural language SQL app showing an error uploading orders with parentheses in the filename" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:55</span>The browser can display the SQLite error, but the server terminal remains silent. The agent cannot use a message trapped in a UI it does not observe.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0835-stdout-fix-diff.jpg" alt="Agent-generated diff adding success and error print statements to FastAPI endpoints" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:55</span>The on-camera agent diff adds detailed error and success prints before returns. This is a demonstration change, not committed source.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The upload path derives a table name from the filename by replacing spaces only. The stem remains <code>orders_(32)</code>. In <code>app/server/core/file_processor.py</code>, line 26 constructs <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code>, which SQLite parses as invalid syntax. The bug is exact. The first lesson, however, is not sanitation; it is visibility.</p></div>
277:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — two short prompts</p><h3>The agent cites the exact statement it observed</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0965-agent-diagnoses-sql-error.jpg" alt="Claude Code diagnosing the SQL error at file_processor.py line 26 after observing server stdout" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">16:05</span>After running the service inside the agent and reproducing the upload, “any issues with the server?” produces a diagnosis naming <code>file_processor.py:26</code>, the PRAGMA statement, and the unsanitized parentheses.</figcaption></figure><p>The sequence is strict. First a new agent is primed and asked to add detailed exception and success prints to <code>server.py</code>. Services restart; the upload proves the trace exists. Another fresh agent is primed and runs <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh, 300s</code>. The browser reproduces the failure while that agent owns stdout. Only then does Dan ask “any issues with the server?” and follow with “resolve: replace bad chars and escape.”</p></div>
293:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — a cold trace</p><h3>Zero conversation context, one precise name</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1220-type-flow-trace-output.jpg" alt="Fresh Claude Code session tracing DatabaseSchemaResponse through five repository files" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">20:20</span>A fresh instance traces <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> through five files. The on-screen Task subagent used seven tool calls, about 26.7k tokens, and 59.2 seconds—evidence that architecture and naming carry context.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan deliberately launches a clean instance and asks how the type flows through the application, requesting files, functions, and purpose. The agent follows the name from its Pydantic definition, through the FastAPI endpoint, across the TypeScript mirror, into the client API call, and back to the original specification.</p></div>
309:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — mirror the tests</p><h3>The tree teaches the next file</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1552-tests-mirror-server-structure.jpg" alt="VS Code explorer showing server core modules mirrored by test files in tests core" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">25:52</span><code>server/core</code> and <code>tests/core</code> mirror one another. The structure tells an agent where a new sanitizer test belongs before it searches.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan names the “agent navigation problem”: every new agent must explore the codebase again. His antidote is consistency—repeat folder patterns, names, comment structures, and responsibilities so each discovered rule predicts the next location.</p></div>
357:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — 749 lines to one shell call</p><h3>The repository shows every rung</h3><div class="frame-grid"><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1812-spec-749-line-plan.jpg" alt="The 749-line natural language SQL interface specification open in the editor" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">30:12</span>The plan is a 749-line prompt containing overview, stack, flow, architecture, types, and implementation detail. Dan emphasizes that he did not hand-type hundreds of plan lines.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1980-claude-p-programmable-mode.jpg" alt="Terminal showing claude -p slash tools returning the tool list and control to the shell" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:00</span><code>claude -p "/tools"</code> runs a reusable prompt headlessly and returns output to the shell. This is the seed of a workflow that code can invoke.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Between the frames sits the template layer. The repo ships <code>install.md</code>, <code>prime.md</code>, and <code>tools.md</code>. After starting the server repeatedly, Dan creates <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> live with one line: <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s</code>. It is created on camera; it is not committed in the source students clone.</p></div>
379:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — write the scorecard</p><h3>Four words replace a vague feeling</h3><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t2072-kpis-size-attempts-streak-presence.jpg" alt="Markdown note listing the four agentic engineering KPIs: Size, Attempts, Streak, Presence" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">34:32</span>The measurement system fits on one screen. The power comes from recording each run and using a broken streak to trigger a missing-lever diagnosis.</figcaption></figure><p>Dan gives concrete ranges. Five-minute runs are a start; push size toward ten, twenty, or thirty minutes, then hours. Attempts sitting around five to ten—or even three to six—suggest missing leverage. Build streaks of three, five, and ten one-shots. Drive presence toward zero so the engineer is not the permanent babysitter.</p></div>

