123:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0183-tactic-adopt-agents-perspective.jpg" alt="Tactic slide for Adopt Your Agent's Perspective with the Core Four litmus question and daily actions" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">3:03</span>The tactic card turns empathy into an engineering checklist: provide the Core Four, readable output, consistent types and architecture, tests, and whatever you would need to do the job.</figcaption></figure></div>
139:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0345-twelve-leverage-points-diagram.jpg" alt="Slide listing the twelve leverage points in in-agent and through-agent families" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">5:45</span>The money slide. The top band is always in-agent; the lower bands are external structures whose information and feedback pass through the agent.</figcaption></figure><div class="leverage-grid" aria-label="Twelve leverage points"><article><strong>Context</strong><span>System prompts, files, history, observations.</span></article><article><strong>Model</strong><span>LLM choice and configuration.</span></article><article><strong>Prompt</strong><span>User instructions and queries.</span></article><article><strong>Tools</strong><span>Available functions and capabilities.</span></article><article><strong>Standard out</strong><span>Command responses and logging.</span></article><article><strong>Types</strong><span>Schemas, classes, errors, structures.</span></article><article><strong>Documentation</strong><span>Internal and third-party knowledge.</span></article><article><strong>Tests</strong><span>Self-validating feedback loops.</span></article><article><strong>Architecture</strong><span>Codebase structure and consistency.</span></article><article><strong>Plans</strong><span>Detailed prompts for massive work.</span></article><article><strong>Templates</strong><span>Reusable, embeddable prompts.</span></article><article><strong>ADWs</strong><span>Prompts, code, and autonomous triggers.</span></article></div></div>
140:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><h3>The demo walks the map instead of lecturing it</h3><p><code>/install</code> and <code>/prime</code> shape context and prompt. <code>/tools</code> exposes capabilities. The planted bug reveals missing stdout. <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> reveals type flow. Client/server and mirrored tests reveal architecture. Local docs reduce lookup cost. Tests close validation. A 749-line spec, reusable slash commands, and <code>claude -p</code> form the abstraction ladder.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0400-install-slash-command-run.jpg" alt="Claude install command reading the README, installing dependencies, and recovering from a failed pip attempt" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:40</span>The install command is already a composition: it executes the prime prompt, reads repository truth, installs both services, and corrects its initial package-manager assumption from the README.</figcaption></figure></div>
155:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0630-tools-list-core-tools.jpg" alt="Course-era core tool list with Task, Bash, search, file, notebook, web, and workflow tools" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">10:30</span>The recorded tool roster is a period detail. The durable move is to inspect names, parameters, side effects, and permission boundaries before asking the agent to work.</figcaption></figure></div>
171:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0715-csv-upload-error-browser.jpg" alt="Natural Language SQL browser showing an error near an opening parenthesis while healthy product results remain visible" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:55</span>The before-state: rich human-visible feedback, silent server. The lesson asks who can observe the error, not whether an error message exists somewhere.</figcaption></figure></div>
172:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The committed <code>server.py</code> has zero <code>print()</code> calls. Upload, query, schema, and insights handlers catch exceptions into response error data; health reports error status without preserving the exception text; delete converts failures to HTTP errors. The routes differ, but the agentic fact is consistent: no detailed handler trace reaches stdout.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0835-stdout-fix-diff.jpg" alt="Agent-generated server diff adding detailed error tracebacks and successful response prints" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">13:55</span>The agent-produced after-state adds readable failure and success evidence before returns. These prints are demonstrated changes; they do not ship in the committed repository.</figcaption></figure></div>
188:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>After instrumentation, a fresh agent primes, runs the service with a five-minute timeout, and observes the failing upload. Dan stops the process and asks only, “any issues with the server?” The answer identifies <code>app/server/core/file_processor.py:26</code>, the <code>PRAGMA table_info</code> call, and the unescaped parentheses. The second prompt—“resolve: replace bad chars and escape”—produces the sanitizer.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t0965-agent-diagnoses-sql-error.jpg" alt="Claude response diagnosing the PRAGMA syntax error in file_processor.py after observing server stdout" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">16:05</span>The payoff is causal, not magical: the agent cites the exact file and line because the system delivered runtime evidence into its context.</figcaption></figure></div>
204:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>In a brand-new instance, Dan asks the agent to show how <code>DatabaseSchemaResponse</code> flows through the app and to detail files, functions, and purpose. A Task subagent searches. The resulting answer names five files and summarizes the flow. The fresh session matters: codebase structure and naming, not conversational memory, carry the trace.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1220-type-flow-trace-output.jpg" alt="Agent output tracing DatabaseSchemaResponse through five files with a Task subagent completion" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">20:20</span>The trace took seven tool uses and about 26.7k tokens on camera. The result is useful because every location can be verified against the repository.</figcaption></figure></div>
251:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1552-tests-mirror-server-structure.jpg" alt="VS Code explorer showing server core files mirrored by tests core files" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">25:52</span>Mirroring turns navigation into a convention: the test for a processor lives where both human and agent expect it.</figcaption></figure></div>
268:        <div class="move move--story"><p class="move-label">3 · Story</p><p>The 749-line specification bootstrapped the app. Three shipped commands preserve installation, priming, and tool introspection. After starting the services three times, Dan asks the agent to create <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> containing the course command text. Then he exits the interactive session and runs <code>claude -p "/tools"</code>, proving the same reusable prompt can be invoked programmatically and return control to the shell.</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1812-spec-749-line-plan.jpg" alt="Top of the 749-line natural-language-to-SQL specification with overview, stack, flow, and architecture" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">30:12</span>A plan is not a tiny preface to coding. Here it is a detailed, reviewable prompt for massive work—and Dan explicitly says he did not hand-type its roughly 750 lines.</figcaption></figure><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t1980-claude-p-programmable-mode.jpg" alt="Terminal showing claude dash p slash tools returning a tool list and control to the shell" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">33:00</span>Programmable mode is the ADW seed: call a reusable prompt headlessly, receive the result, and compose it inside a larger program later.</figcaption></figure></div>
283:        <div class="move move--picture"><p class="move-label">2 · Picture</p><figure class="frame-card"><img src="../media/frames/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/t2072-kpis-size-attempts-streak-presence.jpg" alt="Markdown note listing four agentic coding KPIs: size, attempts, streak, and presence" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">34:32</span>Four words create a compact operating dashboard. The target directions matter as much as the names.</figcaption></figure><div class="kpi-grid"><article class="up"><strong>Size</strong><span>More meaningful work per handoff.</span></article><article class="down"><strong>Attempts</strong><span>Fewer post-completion corrections; aim toward one.</span></article><article class="up"><strong>Streak</strong><span>More consecutive one-shot successes.</span></article><article class="down"><strong>Presence</strong><span>Less live guiding and watching; aim toward zero where safe.</span></article></div></div>
242:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What are the two documentation types and what is <code>ai_docs/</code> for?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Internal repository documentation and external third-party documentation. <code>ai_docs/</code> keeps external references local and agent-readable so every lookup does not require the web.</p></details></div></section></div>
258:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define a closed-loop structure and state Dan’s test warning.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The agent acts, receives concrete validation feedback, and can correct its work. “If you’re not writing tests, you’re probably vibe coding.”</p></details></div></section></div>
274:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Give both formulations of plans and the evidence fact.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Simply, plans are prompts. Accurately, plans communicate massive amounts of work to the agent. The app’s specification is 749 lines and Dan says he did not hand-type it.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">State the three-time rule and the command it produced.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Three times makes a pattern and should trigger automation. Dan created <code>.claude/commands/start.md</code> with the course’s <code>sh ./scripts/start.sh 300s</code> prompt text, enabling <code>/start</code>.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Define an ADW and name its seed mode.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>One or more agentic prompts plus an arbitrary code wrapper plus an autonomous trigger. The seed is programmable mode, demonstrated with <code>claude -p "/tools"</code>.</p></details></div></section></div>
290:        <div class="move move--check"><p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval</p><section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">Name the four KPIs, target directions, and the streak comparison.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Size up, attempts down toward one, streak up, presence down toward zero. Dan contrasts five one-shots with five prompts needing three fixes each and calls the latter roughly three times slower.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">What confession closes the lesson?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The interactive back-and-forth used throughout was still phase-one mode and hurt presence; the course now shifts toward programmable execution while keeping observability and engineering judgment.</p></details></div></section></div>
364:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Reconstruct the SDLC and map one leverage point to each node.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Plan, code, test, review, document. Example mapping: plans → plan; tools → code/action; tests → test; stdout → review evidence; docs → document/next context.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Reconstruct the 4+8 map without looking.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>In-agent: context, model, prompt, tools. Through-agent: stdout, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is the model not the first diagnosis in the planted bug?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The same model succeeds after runtime visibility reaches it. Standard out and tool context, not reasoning capacity, were the demonstrated bottleneck.</p></details></div></section>
366:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Explain the exact filename-to-PRAGMA failure chain.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The upload route derives <code>orders_(32)</code>; pandas creates the table; unquoted <code>PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32))</code> treats parentheses as syntax and fails.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. Why are success logs part of the fix?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>They distinguish verified completion from mere absence of visible failure and give the agent top-level evidence about the path that ran.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">6. What evidence proves the fix rather than merely describes it?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Agent-observed traceback and diagnosis, reviewed diff, focused tests, full green suite, successful upload, and schema showing a valid table with 32 rows and 7 columns.</p></details></div></section>
368:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">7. How do types, architecture, docs, and tests work together on a cold boot?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Docs establish orientation; architecture narrows search; IDK types trace the feature; mirrored tests supply expected behavior and executable feedback.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">8. What is the difference between an agent-readable log and a closed loop?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A log is an observation. A closed loop connects observation to a check and a next corrective action performed by the agent.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">9. Why is “do not refactor yet” part of architecture mastery?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The lesson trains diagnosis and awareness. Premature manual refactoring violates the course’s move toward agent-led systematic change and expands scope before the current levers are learned.</p></details></div></section>
370:        <section class="retrieval-quiz"><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">10. Distinguish a plan, template, and ADW in one sentence each.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A plan communicates one large body of work. A template makes an agentic prompt reusable. An ADW composes prompt(s) in code and launches them through an autonomous trigger.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">11. Attempts rose when size rose. Is the system necessarily worse?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. Larger delegation can temporarily increase attempts. Diagnose the failures, install missing levers, and watch whether attempts fall at the new size.</p></details></div><div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">12. What does presence measure that run duration does not?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>How much live human guiding, watching, and correction the run requires, not merely how long the compute runs.</p></details></div></section>
